CinefexSearchData.register("text/41",{"issue":41,"date":"February 1990","pages":[["1","Cover","number 41 $5.95 Canada - $7.50 cinerex"],["2","Closing pages","H E"],["3","Contents","cinefex CINEFEX 41 - February 1990. Published quarterly at P.O. Box 20027, Riverside, California 92516. Subscriptions (U.S.): four issues - $20; eight issues - $32; twelve issues - $46. Return postage must accompany all unsolicited manuscripts, photos or other materials. Printed by Valley Printers, Riverside, California; color separations by Select Color, Ontario, California. Contents copyright © 1990 by Don Shay. All rights reserved. Reproduction in whole or in part without written permission is prohibited. Jody Duncan Shannon contributing editors Adam Eisenberg circulation manager ...the journal of cinematic illusions editor & publisher Don Shay associate editor Janine Pourroy Ann Webb 4 A Dark and Stormy Knight After a decade of dogged development - with countless scripts proposed and abandoned - Batman finally erupted on the screen last summer amidst a frenzy of hype and merchandising that quickly propelled it high into the ranks of all-time boxoffice champions. The man who cracked the project and finally brought it to fruition was director Tim Burton who surrounded himself with a powerhouse of moviemaking talent. Costume designer Bob Ringwood was enlisted to reconfigure Michael Keaton into the Dark Knight while makeup artist Nick Dudman was similarly engaged to transform Jack Nicholson into the Joker. At the same time, production designer Anton Furst labored to conceive and somely augmented with miniatures created by visual effects supervisor Derek Meddings and mechanical gadgetry provided by physical effects supervisor John Evans. Article by Jody Duncan Shannon. 34 Backyard Odyssey In the surprise megahit of the summer - Honey, I Shrunk the Kidsfour children are accidentally reduced to a quarter-of-an-inch in height and deposited in a backyard jungle where grass and water drops and commonplace insects become horrendous life-threatening obstacles. Spearheaded by director Joe Johnston and executive producer Thomas G. Smith - both veterans of Industrial Light and Magic - the film featured a plethora of giant-size sets and props developed and supplied by production designer Gregg Fonseca and mechanical effects supervisor Peter Chesney. It also entailed a wide range of postproduction miniature and optical illusions accomplished on a shoe-string budget by stopmotion animators Phil Tippett and David Allen and by a diversity of small effects companies including Perpetual Motion Pictures, Visual Concept Engineering and Illusion Arts. The result was a minute comic fantasy on a grand scale. Article by Susan Dayton. FRONT COVER - The nefarious Joker hatches a fiendish plot in Batman. An ant befriends four tiny youngsters in Honey, I Shrunk the Kids- BACK COVER."],["4","Batman","A DARK AND STORMY KNIGHT article by Jody Duncan Shannon"],["5","Batman",""],["6","Batman","6 - CINEFEX 41"],["7","Batman","The idea seemed a natural. It was 1979 - just a few months after Superman began breaking boxoffice records worldwide - when producers Benjamin Melniker and Michael Uslan purchased the movie rights to Batman from DC Comics. The Man of Steel had proved to be a great audience pleaser - why not the Dark Knight? Batman had, after all, been around almost as long - debuting in 1939, only one year after Superman's first comic book appearance. And his popularity had been enduring enough to prompt not only two black-and-white serials in the 1940s, but also a successful - if buffoonish - 1960s television series and several animated Saturday morning children's programs. In many respects, Batman - as originally conceived by illustrator Bob Kane - was a more interesting character than the altruistic Superman. A superhero who was actually devoid of any supernatural powers, Bruce Wayne was a complex individual who used his genius and wealth to pursue a bloodthirsty vigilantism - a 'Dirty Harry' in a batsuit. Even the visual characteristics of Batman and his Gotham City environs seemed well-suited for a cinematic treatment, echoing as they did the film noir style that reached its pinnacle after World War II. If ever a character was meant for transition to the big screen, it was Batman. And yet, nearly ten years would pass between the purchase of the rights and the much-publicized June 1989 opening of the film. The decade of development was fraught with missteps, unfinished scripts, changes in creative personnel and a multitude of wildly differing 'visions' of what the film should be. In spite of its rich cinematic potential - or perhaps because of it - Batman proved to be a tough nut to crack. Under the auspices of Melniker and Uslan, the story was scripted initially by Tom Mankiewicz- a Superman alumnus who approached the Batman legend in a similar fashion, focusing much of the tale on the character's origins as reflected in the witnessing by ten-year-old Bruce Wayne of his parents' brutal murder. When this treatment failed to come to fruition, the project was finally taken up by the production team of Peter Guber and Jon Peters - responsible for such renowned films as The Color Purple, Gorillas in the Mistand Rain Man - with Melniker and Uslan retaining their roles as executive producers. Guber and Peters approached Warner Brothers and the project was given the go-ahead for development - a process that would take yet another nine years. Ten screen treatments were devised, considered and ultimately rejected. Ranging from the 'camp' style of the television series to the outlandish fantasy realm of a Saturday morning cartoon, each of the rejected treatments failed to create the tone the filmmakers were looking for in bringing Batman's story to the screen. While trying to find a satisfactory script, the producers approached several directors to helm the project - including Ivan Reitman and Joe Dante. Expressing more than a casual interest in the film, both directors ultimately had to move on when Batman continued to bog down in the mire of unworkable scripts. The project finally clicked when placed in the unlikely hands of a thirty-year-old former Disney animator with only one major feature film to his credit. Possessing a strong visual style and an offbeat sense of humor, Tim Burton had only recently completed Pee Wee's Big Adventure - a surprise hit, both commercially and critically. After expressing an interest in the Batman project, Burton began weekend collaborations with screenwriter Sam Hamm - a comic book enthusiast whose biggest screen credit to this point was Never Cry Wolf. Though the collaboration was fruitful and unofficially sanctioned by Warner Brothers, it was not until the release of Burton's second feature, Beetlejuiceand the impressive boxoffice numbers which followed - that Warner Brothers committed to the Hamm script with Burton as director. \"I don't think it was a coincidence that the project was greenlighted after the first weekend of Beetlejuice,\" Burton admitted, \"but Warner Brothers has always been supportive of me. From their point of view, Batman was a visual project and they think of me in that context. Because my background is in animation and this was a film based on a comic strip, they seemed to think it was a good connection. Also, the project had been around so long that it had become somewhat of a dinosaur - almost as if it had a curse on it. Everyone said, 'Great project!' But at the same time they were thinking, 'If it's such a great project, why are you having so much trouble getting it made?' So I think I was given the job for all of these reasons - I had an idea as to how it should be done and Sam's script really made it spring to life.\" Working against the deadline of an impending writers' strike, Burton and Hamm scrambled to complete a shootable script. Having thrown out all of the previous attempts, they started from scratch to develop a story that was humorous, yet explored the darker side of Batman and the troubled psyche of Bruce Wayne. \"A lot of the scripts that had been pitched before were either too dark or too light,\" Burton noted. \"I felt it should not be too dark or too campy - I was right in the middle about it. So I pitched 'true' Batman - not the TV series and yet not this extremely dark, unhumorous thing. I thought it could be funny as long as it was not at the expense of the character.\" Script revisions continued well into production, with contributions by Warren Skaaren who would ultimately share screenwriting credit with Hamm - and by Charles McKeown who would not. As finally scripted, the story is set early in Batman's crimefighting career. In the opening moments of the film, the Caped Crusader is not much more than a rumor bandied about in ghost-story fashion by the skeptical criminal element of Gotham City. The police refuse to comment on his existence and the press treats it as a joke - except for journalist Alexander Knox and photographer Vicki Vale, who team up to investigate the mysterious avenger. Soon everyone becomes a believer, however, when Batman puts himself in the middle of a police shootout with mobster Jack Napier at the Axis Chemical factory. Set up by crime boss Carl Grissom, Napier finds himself cornered by Batman and falls eighty feet into a vat of acid. Though presumed dead, Napier emerges from the acid, his face horribly damaged by the toxic chemicals. The botched plastic surgery that follows After fifty years as a comic book superhero, Batman graduated to the big screen last summer with Michael Keaton in the role of millionaire philanthropist Bruce Wayne who disguises himself in a batsuit at night to battle the dark forces of the Gotham City underworld. The Caped Crusader unleashes his speargun - which also fires grappling hooks - one of many specialized gadgets incorporated into a rotating utility belt engineered and constructed by physical effects supervisor John Evans. / The evil Joker - played to the hilt by Jack Nicholson - revels as his plan to wreak lethal havoc on the populace of Gotham City unfolds at the height of a gala bicentennial celebration. / Credited with getting the Batman project on track after ten years of unfocused development is director Tim Burton who managed to mount a darkly humorous production that within weeks of its release became one of the highest grossing films of all time. CINEFEX 41 • 7"],["8","Batman","The film opens on a night view of Gotham City. / The entirely with miniatures and auspices of visual effects supervisor Derek Meddings. miniature bridge - twenty feet long - equipped with red and white tracer lights headlights and taillights. The river underneath was two layers of sliding black miniature tugboats pulled background skyline was a translucent window areas. shot was accomplished scenic art under the In the foreground was a to simulate automobile plastic topped with on hidden wires. The sixty-foot-long scenic painting with backlit 8 * CINEFEX 41 proves to be the last straw for Napier's already questionable mental health - and the Joker is born. Set against the backdrop of the politically unstable Gotham trying to clean up its streets and promote civic pride with the imminent celebration of its bicentennial, the story escalates into an ever-more-deadly contest between Batman and the Joker. Casting the role of Batman proved more difficult than the filmmakers had anticipated. After considering a number of typical leading man types, Jon Peters suggested Michael Keaton - an enormously popular and talented actor who, nevertheless, lacked a classic comic book hero image. Burton liked the idea immediately, but Keaton was mystified at the offer and needed convincing. Eventually he agreed to take the part. The subsequent announcement to the media was met with an astonishing wave of protest from irate Batman fans. Apparently Keaton was not the only one who had difficulty picturing himself in the role. More than fifty thousand angry letters flooded Warner Brothers - a hullabaloo that eventually made the front page of the Wall Street Journal - yet the filmmakers stood by their casting choice. Most of the diehard fans were eventually won over by a hastily, yet effectively, assembled trailer that was shown across \"I think when the fans heard 'Michael Keaton' they thought that we were going to do this really campy thing with the film,\" Burton observed. \"And I knew we were not doing that, so it didn't really upset me. I had kind of anticipated that reaction, anyway, because I've seen it before, When Superman came out, I went to a comic book convention in San Diego where they were doing a slide presentation for the film, and I saw a fan get up and yell: 'Superman would not get up and change on the ledge of a building! I'm going to boycott this movie and tell everyone I know not to go see itl' And people were applauding this guy. So I had seen the passion involved. But it is not as if there is a set mythology with Batman. I had looked at the Batman encyclopedia and found that the mythology contradicts itself - it changes its own history and has gone through many alterations over the years. So early on, I realized that even if I wanted to be true to the 'real' Batman, there could be substantial argument as to what that really was. You could argue ten different concepts and none of them would be wrong. I thought that Michael was a very interesting choice. I had considered a lot of people for the role, but I just could not see them putting on a batsuit. The idea of someone like Michael dressing up as a bat made a lot more sense to me than someone with the classic comic book image. If the guy is handsome and big and strong, it is almost fa one to have more of a joke to have him put on the costume. The character had to have some kind of psychological foundation for doing it, and Michael was perfect because all you have to do is look at him and you get the feeling that he is a little schizophrenic. He brought that quality to the role and I think it worked very well. Michael did for me exactly what I hoped he would do.\" In contrast, the casting of Jack Nicholson as the Joker was an immediately popular choice. Known for his ability to play 'overthe-top' psychotics, Nicholson even had the requisite demonic grin. \"There was a lot of discussion before he actually signed on,\" Burton commented. \"I met with him several times - and the more I met with him, the more I hoped he would do it. What I really admire about Jack is that he is able to find subtext in every role - no matter how wild it is - and make it personal. Our goals were similar in that way - we both wanted to take this thing that was absurd and ridiculous and try to make it feel believable. He is better at that than anybody - the master, really. I think the reason he met with me so many times before committing was that he wanted to be sure what my ideas for the film were. If he had not felt comfortable with me, I don't think he would have done the film. As it was, he really supported me throughout the project.\" With the production set to film at Pinewood Studios in England, Burton and coproducer Chris Kenny began making transatlantic trips to assemble their creative team. \"I wanted to shoot in England for several reasons,\" Burton said. \"It started out as a financial consideration - although soon after we made the decision, the dollar fell and that became a moot point. But, economics aside, England had a lot to offer. Pinewood had the space available - which is difficult to find in L.A. because everything gets booked up. We probably would have wound up having to shoot on location somewhere, and I did not like that idea at all. The characters in the movie are so extreme that I felt it was important to set them in an arena that was specifically designed for them. I liked Superman on an emotional level; but because it was shot on location in New York, I don't think it captured the visual quality of the comic book. The thought of shooting Batman in some New York location just did not feel right to me. Pinewood had a perfect setup for building the sets we needed - a big outdoor area with a street already laid in. So all of the groundwork had already been laid and we designed Gotham City with that space in mind. I also wanted to shoot there because with a big-budget movie there is so much hype and pressure. I felt I would be able to focus better if I was away from all of that Burton and Kenny first secured the services of production designer Anton Furst. Having unsuccessfully tried to retain him for Beetlejuice, Burton was elated when Furst agreed to participate on Batman. \"Design is always very important to me,\" said Burton, \"and there are very few film designs that I get excited about. I had liked The Company of Wolves - which Anton designed - and I thought that he was one of the most individual designers around. Because I tend to have very strong thoughts on design, I love working with people who give me something. I like to be surprised. Anton does that and he also gets very involved in every aspect of the film - which was especially great for me because I had so much to handle on this one.\" The collaboration with Burton proved to be satisfying for Furst, as well. \"Within a short period of time, we were able to develop a language for the film that we both understood. And it was uncanny - I would come up with six designs and Tim would"],["9","Batman","invariably go for the one I liked best. So we were definitely in tune. Because he is so visually aware, he supported the best things that I did rather than confusing the issue. It was really one of the best relationships with a director I have ever had.\" Signing on to the project in February 1988, Furst had only three months in which to complete all of the designs for the film - a task that included the batmobile, batwing and all of Gotham City. Gotham was to be realized through a combination of miniatures and a massive full-scale set erected on a five-anda-half-block stretch of the Pinewood backlot. \"The skyscrapers of the city are supposed to be up to one thousand feet high,\" Furst explained, \"but from the beginning, I knew that we were only going to be building, at most, the first fifty feet of the buildings in full-scale. The 'top' of the entire city was slated to be done with models. By far the biggest challenge was figuring out what should be built full-scale and how we could best achieve enormously complicated effects within the infrastructure of fullscale sets along with model sets in several different scales. It was very difficult.\" With construction of the full-scale Gotham City set targeted to begin in May, the production designer first tackled the problems inherent in devising a fictional city, \"The most important thing was that we find a feel for the city which was neither futuristic nor historical. We wanted it to be as timeless as possible, even though - since we were drawing from the original DC comic strip for inspiration - there was bound to be a certain '40s feeling to it. So starting with the look of Gotham in the comic strip, we then combined that with the worst aspects of New York City - imagining what might have happened to New York had there been no planning commission and had it been run by pure extortion and crime. For example, we completely threw out any concept of zoning or construction laws that insure skyscrapers are built so that light will still fall on the streets below. Instead, we maximized space, bridged over streets, built the buildings cantilevering toward the streets rather than away from them - absolutely no respect whatsoever for city planning, As Tim characterized it, it was just a hell that had erupted through the pavement and kept on growing.\" Furst also tried to incorporate an eclectic look to the city. \"That is really more realistic, anyway. Metropolis looks as if one person designed the whole town. But New York or any other real metropolis looks as if it is something that has been designed by thousands of architects over hundreds of years. So we went for a potpourri of different styles within the same complex, being careful to control it so that in the end we would have a cohesive composition. I chose styles which, when put together, would create its own kind of style - everything from the early brownstone buildings to modern brutalism, gothic architecture to Italian futurism. We even tapped facism for the city hall. For the Flugelheim Museum, I chose a modern Japanese architect named Shin Takamatsu who does this really brutal, locomotive type architecture - it has the look of nuts and bolts and castings and that sort of thing. So we put together this dadaistic CINFFEX 41 • 9"],["10","Batman","10 - CINEFEX 41 MONARCH"],["11","Batman","potpourri and we became quite comfortable with it. We began to formulate our own language of architecture.\" Because most of the action of the film was to take place at night, Furst rejected detailed ornamentation in favor of simpler, bolder silhouettes. \"Masses of small ornaments just would not have been seen; so we went for huge, powerful graphic shapes. Also, because I wanted the Joker character to be the main source of color in the film, we made the city very starved of color. It is colorless and dank and dreary and really just a miserable place.\" As the centerpiece of the town - as well as the setting for Batman's final confrontation with the Joker - the cathedral was one of the most important elements of the Gotham City de- \"We had these other broad-stroked, straight-lined structures,\" Furst said, \"so when we came to the cathedral, I thought we should offset that with an organic building devoid of any straight lines. And again, I wanted to avoid a connection with any particular time period. I had always been fascinated by the work of Antoni Gaudi - a turn-of-the-century architect. There is a gothic feeling to it, and yet it is not gothic. It is very difficult to position in time, which made it perfect for the cathedral. I've also always liked the house in Hitchcock's Psycho, so I topped off the cathedral with the top of the Hitchcock house and we ended up with this really eccentric building with deep-pitched gothic roofs and spires coming off the top. It really had to be a call ben to bed forbidding looking thing in my view - as if it had been closed down and locked up because God had left the city years ago.\" Ultimately, Furst's grand vision of Gotham City had to be constructed in three dimensions - a huge undertaking that required most of Pinewood's ninety-five-acre backlot, as well as the majority of its eighteen sound stages. \"I had a wonderful construction manager, Terry Apsey, who's done huge films like Doctor Zhivago and other David Lean pictures. So he is a very competent man accustomed to working on this kind of grand scale. But it was pretty hairy anyway, building sets fifty feet high, half a mile long and then plastering and painting all of that. Also, the way I had designed it — with the buildings cantilevering forward - made the construction a bit more difficult because the preparation below the ground level had to be almost as substantial as what we built aboveground. All of the footings and bearings had to be set into concrete. And because it was so high, we had to build the set to withstand up to forceeight wind conditions - and that is tons of weight.\" Not only was it a huge set - the biggest outdoor movie set built in Europe since Cleopatra - it had to be completed in a relatively short amount of time. \"Because it was outdoors, it was important that we begin shooting before winter set in. So we were really pushed to finish it as quickly as possible. It took us five months to build it - with about two-hundred-and-fifty men working on it at one point. It was a hard summer.\" While the massive backlot set was being built, Furst was also directing the construction of several soundstage sets - most notably, the batcave which was built on the largest stage at Pinewood. Furst had found the design process a difficult one. \"Caves are such boring, amorphous looking things. In trying to find an interesting approach to it, I came up with a Phantom of the Opera concept whereby the batcave is actually in the foundations of the city with the piles of the skyscrapers and sewer pipes and things like that coming down through the rock structures. That made it more interesting and also more dangerous - which fit in with Batman because everything he does is on the edge of disaster. Our batcave had precipices that seemed to go on forever - you never knew just how deep they were. It was designed to be a huge chasm in the ground, but obviously even the biggest soundstage is only so big. So we built the practical areas - the area with Batman's panel of gadgets and the catwalks and the long ramp that the batmobile drives up on - and then matted in the rest with scenic art.\" Design work on a much less grandiose scale was in progress at the same time as costume designer Bob Ringwood attempted to realize the definitive batsuit. Ringwood - who contributed extraordinary designs for Excalibur and Dune and earned an Academy Award nomination for Empire of the Sun - had just turned down the current James Bond film the day before Chris Kenny approached him regarding Batman. \"The Bond film was going to be shooting in Mexico for a long time,\" Ringwood explained, \"and since my mother was ill at the time, I just did not feel I should leave England. So the Batman job was ideal for me - and it was really just by chance that I was available to do it. I thought it was a very interesting proposition because it was a chance to bring a two-dimensional character to life. In cartoons you can draw anything - you can increase the size of the cape for dramatic effect, for example, or change the shape or make it smaller. Dealing with the physical problems of doing that in three dimensions on a real person is a nightmare. But I was eager to do it because I thought it would be an interesting challenge to compete with the cartoons.\" In accepting the assignment, Ringwood was taking on the responsibility of helping to create the Batman aura. \"Bob is a real artist,\" Burton commented, \"and he was able to hook into what Anton and I were doing right away. He got into the psychology of whya guy would put on a batsuit - the fact that it was partly for protection and partly for theatrical effect. The justification for the costume was extremely well thought out. He was also able to give it a certain kinky quality, too - the rubbery, leather kind of thing - that I really liked. He made the costume work - both for the film and for Michael.\" Though he had signed on to the project very early, Ringwood had been reluctant to even begin designing the bat costume until he knew for certain who was going to be playing the role. \"I imagined it was going to be a big six-foot-four hunk with a dimpled chin. Michael Keaton is many things, but one thing he is not is a six-foot-four hunk. It was interesting casting; but because he is average height and a small-built guy, it was a surprise and just a bit scary for me. I wasn't sure how to interpret it. The whole essence of the Batman character in the comics is this huge guy - like all the superheroes. So the problem was to Rather than shoot on location, Tim Burton felt strongly that Gotham should be designed and constructed from scratch to give it a distinctive character in keeping with its comic book origins. With an eye toward emphasizing the oppressive consequences of uncontrolled urban development, production designer Anton Furst converted much of the ninety-five-acre backlot at Pinewood Studios into a street set that would be extended - laterally and vertically - via miniatures and matte paintings. / Storyboard artist Michael White polishes a pencil rendering that would later serve as a blueprint for the opening cityscape shot. / The production prepares for a night scene. Five hectic months were devoted to building the quarter-milelong street set. CINEFEX 41 • 11"],["12","Batman","batsuit that doubled as body outer suit configuration was deemphasize the roundness headpiece was constructed with aluminum cheek plates that gave the actor a more chiseled physiognomy. / In the cathedral bell tower high above the streets of Gotham, Batman prepares to launch battle with the Joker and his minions. / Batman bursts through the skylight of the Flugelheim Museum to free Vicki Vale. Though extensive and time consuming to rig provided by John Evans - was simplified by the fact Costume designer Bob Ringwood was able to suggest an impressive physique for the slightly built Michael Keaton by devising a formidable armor. Over a fiberglass replica of the actor, the sculpted in clay, then molded and cast in foam rubber sections. To of Keaton's face, the himself into a climactic wire work for the film - that there was no requirement to conceal the wires. 12 • CINEFEX 41 make somebody who was average-sized and ordinary looking into this bigger-than-life creature. Ultimately what we decided to do was to not make him bigger than life, but to make him as much visually like Batman as we could and not worry about the scale. Because Michael is not a real big guy, one of my concerns was that we not make a costume that was so big and so muscular that it would just swamp him. If we had done that, he would been digest. not have been able to give a performance - he just would have been lumbering around in sixteen tons of rubber. Ultimately, the thickest part of the costume was only about two-and-aquarter inches, and that was around the shoulder area. All I really did with the costume was streamline Michael's body - instead of having him go to a gym for two years. And I think we ended up with a more interesting costume than we would have had if there had been a muscular guy underneath. We were able to exaggerate muscles and stylize them, whereas on a real body we would have had just real muscles.\" Once the casting of the role was locked, Ringwood - who had not previously been a Batman fan - began doing extensive research. \"I had seen one or two of the television episodes when I was a kid, but I had never even read a Batman comic. So I went out and bought about two hundred of them from all the different time periods - including the really early ones - and studied them thoroughly, From talking to Tim Burton, I knew that he wanted to make this Batman more the Dark Knight of the later comics and lose the camp TV elements and also lose the sort of gauche style of the early cartoons. So I looked at more of the recent comics than the others and based the look of the costume on those, hoping we could recreate the Dark Knight look in three dimensions. Then I closed the comics and started thinking about why a man would dress up as a bat - the whole idea is absurd - and I decided that to make it work I had to come up with a bat that was dark and mysterious and sexy. At that point, I forgot the comic and went my own way - although in the back of my mind always was the fact that it had to relate to the comic in some way.\" One of the things that troubled Ringwood about both the TV series and the comic strip drawings was the fact that Batman's costume was in shades of blue. \"I had decided from the beginning that this Batman was not going to be in blue knickers. I hated those. Bats are black, of course - not blue - and black is much more sinister and sexy. After talking to Bob Kane, we found out that he had always thought of Batman as being in black, but that it was very difficult to draw a black-on-black drawing for the comic strip. So he had drawn it in blue so that he could use different tones of the color for effect. In his mind, the blue was just a symbolic version of black. Our black costume was really nearer the original concept.\" After completing an airbrushed drawing of the costume, Ringwood sent an assistant to Canada - where Keaton was shooting a film at the time - to take head and body casts of the actor. From those molds, a positive cast of Keaton's body was made in fiberglass. \"It was like having Michael here in the studio - only in fiberglass. I spent a week with Lynne Burman - who is a wonderful sculptress - building up this fiberglass body with clay, trying to get a dynamic shape out of the body. We rejected several of them - some got far too big and looked almost like the Incredible Hulk - but finally we came down to a streamlined aerodynamic version of the muscular body.\" Using the finalized clay sculpture, molds were made and sections of the suit were cast in foam rubber. All of the separate sections were then attached to a cotton-lycra bodysuit that had been treated to give it a matching rubberized surface. \"We used only the best quality foam rubber so that the costume could withstand the enormous wear and tear it would go through during filming. The bodysuit was very tight fitting and all of the musculature was glued onto it separately. So, even though it looked like it was one piece, it was actually made up of many parate part was separate parts - a chest piece, upper arm, lower arm, a crotch and side piece, kneecaps and the lower leg. It had a zipper in the back so that Michael could just step into it.\" Because all of Batman's appearances in the film would be at night, Ringwood and his crew polished the completed suit with silicone - a process that had to be repeated throughout filming - so that what little light was available would be picked up and reflected off the musculature. In all, twenty-eight of the bodysuits were constructed to accommodate not only Michael Keaton but also the various stunt performers employed in the film. Satisfied with the bodysuit, Ringwood turned his attention to Batman's headpiece. \"I really had to go back to the comics for the head because that silhouette was the thing that really said 'Batman.' And it turned out to be the most complicated part of it, really. For one thing, we found that in the drawings the head was cheated a little. They would draw him full-face or threequarters or from the back - but those things put together did not really add up to a three-dimensional head. So if we tried to sculpt the front like it was in the drawing, then the sides and the back would not look very good. And if we sculpted the back of it right, then the front would change and it would not look correct. So we had to sculpt the head again and again and again, and what we ended up with was the best composite result.\" Keaton's bone structure presented some difficulties in sculpting the headpiece. \"Michael has a rather round face,\" Ringwood commented, \"yet the image of Batman is this very sculptured, chiseled creature. So we had to 'chisel' Michael's features with the shape of the headpiece. We did that by putting aluminum cheek plates inside the mask each time he put it on. They were held fast to his face by the tight rubber and they helped to give him a chiseled look. Another thing we did was make the ears as long and pointed as possible. The ears really have no function at all when you think about it. He doesn't hear through them and he doesn't fly with them - they are purely visual. So I figured they were a bit like the 'go-faster' fins on 1950s American cars - just nonsense, really, to make him look mean and fast - and I tried to stylize them that way. It was a bit of a problem because the cockpit of the batmobile had to be high enough to accom-"],["13","Batman","CINEFEX 41 • 13"],["14","Batman","14 • CINEFEX 41"],["15","Batman","modate them. But they had to look tall and elegant, as if they were aerodynamic. Otherwise they were just ludicrous things with no function - like in the TV program where he wore those silly little mouse ears.\" As drawn in the comic strip, Batman's eyes are merely expressionless white slits. \"We tried blanking out his eyes with screens to match the drawings, but that left Michael with only his mouth to act with. Since the eyes are the actor's primary tools, we felt it was better to leave the eye sockets open. To minimize any problems he might have seeing out of the mask, we made the area around the eyes as thin as possible. The foam rubber was only about an eighth-of-an-inch thick in that area - close enough to his face to avoid any visual problems. His peripheral vision was a bit restricted, but that turned out to be kind of a good thing - it forced him to move in a rather butch sort of way because he had to turn his entire body in order to see to the side. It gave him a powerful, definite kind of movement. Instead of just his head moving, the whole body swings around with the cape sweeping out behind him.\" Ringwood had originally planned to make the headpiece and the cape as one unit, but reconsidered when it became apparent that the headpiece was particularly vulnerable to damage. To avoid having to replace the cape every time the headpiece was replaced - which was often - the two were made separately, then attached each time the suit was put on by gluing a scallopshaped collar at the bottom of the headpiece onto the shoulder section of the cape. The cape itself - though seemingly the least complicated part of the Batman costume - was actually the most problematic. \"The cape was by far the most difficult thing to construct. We tried making a cape of pure rubber - to match the rubberized look of the rest of the costume - but we found that it moved in a very unattractive way, rippling rather than swinging. So then we did a variety of tests with different fabrics, trying to find something that would move in the way we wanted. What we came up with was a venetian wool that is very heavy and luxurious and expensive. To match it to the rest of the costume, we sprayed wet rubber onto glass and then vary carefully laid the woolen fabric down and smoothed it out with rollers. After the rubber set, we would peel the whole thing off the glass and the fabric would be bonded to the back of it. The only problem with it was that we could not sew it together. The cape had been constructed in segments like an orange - each piece going out from the center - but we could not sew those sections together without getting terribly heavy, clutzy-looking seams. So we got Paul Barrett Brown, who is a rubber specialist - he makes rubber fetish clothes for the sex market, so he knew an awful lot about rubber - and we put this problem to him. He suggested that we make an enormous table covered in highly polished vinyl. The table was fourteen feet in diameter and, like the cape, was constructed in orangelike segments. We sprayed the rubber on this enormous table and the fabric was cut to fit each segment exactly - without any overlap - so the seams just butted up together. So it was the rubber itself that bonded the cape. We then reinforced the seam areas just slightly with a very fine piece of silk gauze which was laid right into the rubber. That worked beautifully - we were able to construct this enormous cape without any sewing on it at all.\" Because of its weight, attaching the cape to the costume required extensive hardware. \"Michael wore a body harness underneath the costume,\" Ringwood explained. \"The body harness had two bolts at the shoulders and the cape was literally screwed into those bolts on each side. The hardware was covered by the bat symbol which was bolted onto the chest. So there was a lot of understructure holding the cape in place - if there had not been, the weight of it when he swung around would have torn the entire costume right off of his body.\" Once the prototype was perfected, several capes of varying lengths were produced. \"We made thirty-five or forty capes all together. Some of them were about five feet long, reaching from Michael's neck to the ground. Others were a bit shorter so that he could get in and out of the batmobile easily. Then, for shots where he is flying up a wire or floating down into a scene, we made capes that were eight to ten feet long, just for the visual effect. We used a a lighter-weight material for those so that the cape would lift and flow nicely in flight. In some instances we even used a parachute material.\" Gloves and a pair of boots completed the costume. \"The gloves had more 'go-faster' fins on them, which were made of rubber and flexible so that they would not harm him as he rolled around and did his stunts. We patterned them from a motorcycle glove which was made of leather and very butch looking. Those were simple, but I was very concerned about the boots. Very few men have thick ankles, and if you do not have a thick ankle and you have a very muscular body on top, you can look weak and out of proportion. The comic just has a simple boot - like a riding boot - but I did not think that would translate well on screen. The look I liked best was big training shoes. There were some Nike training shoes that were butch looking - big things with straps on them - so I contacted Nike in Oregon and they agreed to design a bat boot based on one of their training shoes. I did some drawings, sent them off to Nike and they did up a prototype. The boots were rubber and leather and there was a piece that went over the ankle - like a riding stirrup - to thicken up the ankle.\" Donning the complete costume was a complicated procedure that took up to two-and-a-half hours. \"After some practice, we got it down to about twenty minutes. But there was quite a lot to it - the body harness had to be put on first, then the bodysuit, then all of the bolts had to be screwed in. Finally the headpiece was put on. Obviously - because it was such an ordeal - once Michael was in the costume, he had to stay in it until they were through shooting for the day. I had thought perspiration would be a problem; but luckily, it was not Michael's nature to perspire much. Also, we had left a few areas of the lycra suit 'unrubberized' so that air could flow through to make it a bit Working over plaster castings of Jack Nicholson - who had contractual approval over his appearance in the film - prosthetic makeup artist Nick Dudman sculpted six slightly varying concepts for the Joker. When the actor made his choice - the same one favored by both Burton and Dudman - latex rubber appliance pieces were manufactured in quantity to provide a sufficient supply for the film. / Although his natural skin color is a strident white, the Joker sometimes tries to conceal his abnormality by applying flesh-tone makeup. Dudman performs some on-set touchup work as Nicholson prepares for the scene in which his character seizes control of the Gotham City crime syndicate. / A twohour makeup procedure was required whenever Nicholson had to appear as the Joker. One of the most arduous aspects was creating a smooth blend between the appliance pieces and the actor's real flesh - a process made difficult by the white makeup which accentuated even the smallest of creases. CINEFEX 41 • 15"],["16","Batman","Keaton confer between takes cathedral set. / Designed by Anton Furst, the batmobile was constructed in duplicate by John Evans and his crew. were extensively modified racing car bodies fashioned from high-impact kevlar. Functional machine guns and jet afterbumers were incorporated into the vehicle controlled by the driver of effects technician. / Batman and Vicki Vale dash from the museum to the batmobile parked outside. / Among its vehicle can shield itself on verbal command. Though animation, Evans and his crew constructed a mock steel-plated batmobile for use on the live-action set. Tim Burton and Michael on the smoke-filled A junkyard provided two Chevrolet chassis which and encased in custom as well. / Most of the practical weaponry was the vehicle - usually a stuntman or a special varied capabilities, the the actual process was accomplished via cel 16 - CINEFEX 41 more comfortable. After he was finished for the day, we would get Michael out of the suit - which was another long process because the headpiece had to be unglued from the shoulder and cape - and then hang the costume up to air out.\" Though Ringwood had labored to make the costume as comfortable for Keaton as possible, there were inherent discomforts in the heavy rubberized suit. \"I put the head on myself,\" Tim Burton noted, \"and it really was a bizarre feeling. I could not hear and my peripheral vision was affected. Michael is a bit claustrophobic, too, which made it worse for him. The costume put him in a dark, Batman-like mood though, so he was able to use it to his advantage.\" Moving from the sublime to the ridiculous, Ringwood also devised the outrageously flashy costumes worn by the Joker. \"I tried to be extremely conventional with the Joker costume designs - which sounds absurd when you consider what they look like. But they are really just very good English tailoring done in the most bizarre colors. I did not want the costumes to be so extreme that there was nothing left for Jack to do - that he would walk on screen and the costume would give the performance for him. So I really tried to control it by using classically cut clothes in very unusual colors.\" In the case of the Joker, however, clothes most definitely did not make the man. Makeup designer Nick Dudman - who had worked previously with Anton Furst on High Spirits- was brought in early in preproduction to devise a makeup for the famous Joker grin. Since Jack Nicholson's contract had given him final approval of the makeup design, Dudman first interviewed the actor to review the various options. \"We discussed the basic ways that we could approach the problem. Should we do it with dental appliances behind his lips that would pull his mouth back? Or should we do it with just a prosthetic makeup with him in a relaxed expression? If we put a dental appliance in, would that affect his speech? Because he had never done anything with prosthetic makeups before, he had some general concerns. I got as much input from him as I could so that when he came over I would have something to show him that would fit in with his ideas.\" After the preliminary interview, Dudman did a lifecast of the actor's face. \"I went back to England with the lifecast and sculpted six different concepts that were based on the comic strip and, of course, Jack Nicholson's face. The six designs were not vastly different. The character is so laid down in the strip that we could not stray too far from that look. There was always the concern that we not offend the comic book fans, yet we also did not want to lose Jack Nicholson in the makeup. The sculpting had to be very subtle, and that was the most difficult part of the job - trying to get the well-known Joker character without obliterating Nicholson's face. Unlike the usual prosthetic makeup job where you are accentuating things, this makeup required deaccentuation. Ultimately, the six designs ran the gamut from the face being pulled back into a horrifying manic leer at one extreme to just the slightest alteration of Nicholson's face at"],["17","Batman","CINEFEX 41 • 17"],["18","Batman","18 - CINEFEX 41"],["19","Batman","the other - and then everything in between. It was up to Jack Nicholson to choose the one that he liked.\" Because Nicholson was due in a mere two weeks before shooting was to begin, Dudman made up the latex rubber prosthetic pieces for all six of the different concepts so that they would be prepared to move forward with whichever one the actor selected. \"That way we knew that we could do at least a week-anda-half's shooting with the makeup, during which time we could reproduce our molds for whichever design he went for. Fortunately, he chose the one that Tim and I both liked best.\" Though creating the prosthetic pieces for the makeup was a fairly straightforward procedure, the Joker's unusual coloring was a matter of some concern for Dudman and required numerous makeup tests before Nicholson's arrival. \"One of the things that had me worried from the beginning was the Joker's green hair. Green dyed hair very often goes brown on film, taking on a dull muddy tone. So we had to come up with a green color that would photograph well - which was pretty tricky. Luckily, the hairdresser on the film - Colin Jamison - is probably the top person in the field. He went to work testing greens on all types of hair and finally pinned that down.\" Another major concern for the makeup team was the Joker's acid-blanched skin. \"It is a problem to shoot someone whose skin is that white on a dark set - especially when the protagonist is in a black costume. Lighting for the dark background and the black Batman would just wipe out any subtlety on the Joker. So we had to come up with a way of shading and highlighting his white skin so that we retained some definition on his face - otherwise it would just burn out on film. We wound up using an iridescent white acrylic paint as the base and over that we put a pure white camouflage cream - as opposed to a makeup product - because the pigment content is so much higher. Then the shading was done with iridescent eyeshadow colors dissolved in alcohol. The eyeshadow colors soaked into the acrylic paint base underneath the makeup and bonded to it so they did not blur or travel as time went on.\" The actual application of the makeup required two hours. \"The entire process consisted of applying two upper lip pieces that had to be put on while Nicholson was grinning so that they were under the correct tension. Then, over those went two pieces - one on either side - that ran from the cheek and incorporated the cheekbone and the nasolabial folds coming down into the lower jaw to provide the lower lip of the smile. That piece was fixed with a locking point into the upper lip prosthetic. The locking point was just a little plug that was negative on the upper lip piece and positive on the piece that came over it so that it literally snapped in. Because he had to talk and there was so much facial movement, we could not take the risk of just gluing the pieces down - they would have come loose too easily. Since it was foam, the locking point did not physically hold it together, but it helped and it gave us a locating point so that the piece would always fit in correctly.\" The next step was the application of the chin piece. \"That was applied under tension, Batman tries to outrun the explosive charges he has planted at the Axis Chemical factory where the Joker is manufacturing toxic materials specifically to decimate the local populace. Live-action portions of the sequence were photographed at an abandoned power station outside London where John Evans was turned loose with instructions to orchestrate a massive conflagration. / Wide shots of the entire plant exploding were produced on stage by Derek Meddings and company. A six-foot-tall model was constructed and prescored so that it would blow apart in a predictable manner. / Meddings attends to the wiring leading from the explosives within the model to the detonation devices controlled manually from a safe distance. / The explosion was photographed at five times normal speed and then split-screened into a shot of the batmobile speeding out of the Acton location site. CINEFEX 41 • 19"],["20","Batman","Anton Furst adds finishing details to his design for the Axis Chemical plant exterior. batcave as being beneath building foundations and principal set - constructed represented only a small portion of the full batcave. The more expansive aspects establishing shots via matte command console - Bruce Wayne studies a televised / Furst envisioned the the city, nestled precariously amongst pipes and conduits. The on stage at Pinewood - of the hideaway were incorporated into paintings. / Inside the batcave - seated at his image of the Joker as he grapples with haunting memories from his past. 20 - CINEFEX 41 also. Everything had to be done that way, because when he was acting he was actually holding the grin himself underneath the foam. We then applied a nose tip - which was just a small piece of foam - and that was all that was required. So it was not a huge job in terms of the number of pieces. The trick was losing the edges into his own skin - which was surprisingly smooth - and then making up over the top in white which tends to make everything show up. We spent a lot more time working with the surface of the pieces than we normally have to do with a flesh-colored makeup.\" Complicating the surface of the makeup was the fact that the Joker - in a somewhat fruitless attempt to look 'normal' - covers his white skin with a flesh-toned makeup before his lunch date with Vicki Vale at the Flugelheim Museum. In her struggle to escape the madman, Vicki throws a pitcher of water at him and his grotesque coloring is revealed. \"We did the complete white Joker makeup, then sprayed him with a silicone spray that is used in cooking, which acted as a separator. Next we covered the white makeup with a flesh-toned greasepaint. He had sponges soaked in alcohol in his palms so when he was hit by the water, he put his hands up and wiped his face with the sponges. The alcohol streaked the flesh-colored greasepaint off, Enduring the tedious, almost daily application of the makeup for four months, Nicholson proved to be a patient subject - much to Nick Dudman's relief. \"I had expected someone of Jack Nicholson's stature to complain quite a bit about having to sit still for two hours every morning - especially since he does not normally wear any makeup at all in films. But he was as good as gold to us - one of the most professional people I have ever worked with.\" While the Joker is a formidable adversary, he eventually succumbs to Batman and his amazing array of unique combat weapons. An integral part of the Batman legend from its inception, the 'bat gadgets' - the batmobile, batwing and utility belt - were designed by Anton Furst and realized by physical effects supervisor John Evans. An esteemed alumnus of the James Bond series, Evans was eminently qualified to take on the job of creating dynamic and fully-functioning props for the film. Arguably the most popular feature in Batman's arsenal, the batmobile presented the filmmakers with their biggest challenge. \"The batmobile worried us a lot,\" Burton admitted. \"Early on, it seemed that everything Anton and I came up with just looked like we were putting fins on cars. Cars can so easily become a joke - people don't realize how difficult it is to make a new kind of car. After a long period of time in which Anton reworked and refined and adjusted the design, we finally hit on something that looked good. Even after the drawing was done, though, we still were not sure how it would go over in the film. It wasn't until we saw it in rushes that we knew it was right.\" Envisioning the batmobile as a kind of shining armor for the Dark Knight, Furst designed the vehicle as an extension of Batman himself. \"I never really saw the batmobile as just a car,\" Furst said. \"I always thought of it as an appendage to Batman's exterior - just like his cape or his suit. I was really scared of the idea of it becoming a sort of pimpmobile - you see so many of those low-level concept cars in films. To me, the batmobile was a pure piece of expressionism. I tried to give it that 'knight in armor' look, taking elements of the speed machines from the Utah salt flats of the '40s, the Corvette stingrays of the '50s and combining those elements with jet aircraft components to create one cohesive machine.\" Because the batmobile would have to perform in the film as more than just a good-looking prop, Furst had to confer with Evans to ensure that the design was not just visually exciting, but practical as well. \"The cars had to function not only as performance cars - with machine guns and afterburners and all that sort of thing laid into them - but also as driving machines,\" Furst commented. \"It takes ten years for General Motors to produce a new car and we had to do this in five months! So I had to design it relatively quickly so that it could get off the drawing board and into the workshops and be ready to shoot. In a way, the batmobile was a more hairy project than building those huge sets. It certainly worried me the most, because it was such an important element of the film. It would have been terrible to come up with a batmobile that was weak.\" Though Furst worked furiously to complete the design as quickly as possible, time constraints forced John Evans to begin constructing the batmobile without a finished concept. \"We had only twelve weeks to build two batmobiles - one for stunt work and one for principal photography,\" Evans noted. \"But Anton still was not quite happy with the design at that point. So he gave us an outline and we had to go from there. I just could not afford to wait. Because we were so short on time, I felt that I had to get a chassis that was proven, rather than experiment with our own. So I went out and found two 1974 V-8 Chevrolet Impala chassis at a junkyard. They were solid and had nice suspension. We then modified the chassis to fit our specific needs - we lengthened them by two feet, packed the wheels out four inches and dropped the engine about twelve inches. We also put a new suspension in and fitted the chassis with twenty-four-inch-width Mickey Thompson drag racing tires. Then we started from there. It was a tricky period, because we had to go ahead and build it even though we did not have the final measurements and we still did not know exactly what gadgets were going to be inWhile Evans and his crew worked on modifying the Impala chassis, Furst completed the cosmetic aspects of the design. Having found it difficult to render the three-dimensional concept in drawings, the production designer had moved quickly into maquette form. Fashioned out of clay, the maquette was two feet long and six inches high. Set modeler Eddie Butler sculpted the approved rendering up to full-scale - twenty-three feet long and almost nine feet wide - working in high-density polystyrene. The full-scale polystyrene pieces then went to a company that specializes in building Le Mans racing cars to be"],["21","Batman","CINEFEX 41 • 21"],["22","Batman","22 • CINEFEX 41 • E"],["23","Batman","prepared for molding. \"The batmobile was built in kevlar,\" Furst noted, \"which is a polycarbonate that is extremely light and yet very strong. It is the same material used to make racing cars. We then had a custom paint job done on it. There were about six different layers of colors to give the car the finish of a beetle - a lot of colors coming through the blackness so that it had a three-dimensional quality to it rather than being just a flat - we beit with a slight black. We did not go to a full gloss on it - we left it with a slight matte finish to make it look more like a war machine.\" Fitted with interior components from fighter aircraft and an array of military-strength guns and explosives, the batmobile did more than just look like a war machine. \"We had two 9mm Browning machine guns which came out of either side on air rams and fired out dummy bullets,\" Evans explained. \"All of the controls for the guns were actually inside the car. We had speed regulators on them so that they could go at whatever speed the director wanted at the time. The flaps of the car were on compressed air so that they could fly up and off about twenty feet into the air. There was also a grappling hook which fired out of the side which was powered by compressed air and rams. The most difficult thing about all of this was just finding room for all the air cylinders and reservoirs and everything. The inside of the batmobile was very small and it was quite a tight fit just to get everything we needed in there.\" Adding to the batmobile's visual allure were rocket engine flames shooting out the back of the car. \"That was a tricky situation,\" Evans noted. \"To make it look effective on film, we had to have a lot of flame - which, of course, generated a tremendous amount of heat. We insulated the back of the car and also put CO, extinguishers in a ring around it, just in case we had an accident with the fire. The extinguishers were on a panic button which we put both inside and outside of the car. That way, if the artist got caught inside, he had the capability of employing the extinguishers. The flames were generated with a mixture of gas and air and pyrothane.\" The only nonfunctional part of the batmobile were the armor shields that open and close at Batman's verbal command. Although the shields were physically incorporated into the vehicle, the opening and closing effect was generated through animation. Depending on the level of danger in a particular shot, the batmobile was driven either by Bob Marlais - a member of the Evans effects crew - or a stuntman. \"Bob drove it when there were gadgets involved or there was a timing problem for special effects,\" explained Evans, \"but if it got too dangerous, a stuntman took over. In some instances where it was crucial that we hit a certain mark at a certain speed, we had to have one of our guys drive it just so the timing was right. One of our first takes - where the batmobile is racing towards the doors of the chemical plant and there are bullet hits going across the door just as the batmobile gets there - was also one of the most difficult. There was only an inch to spare under the bullet hits and the batmobile had to go through there just perfectly. So I had my man drive the car for that sequence and we got it on the first take. That started us off well with the director.\" Difficulties notwithstanding, Evans is understandably proud of his automotive achievement. \"We managed to deliver two batmobiles for only about 151,000 pounds - which is not bad. And they were sound automobiles. There were no mechanical breakdowns on either car during the entire project. Although the batmobile never went faster than sixty miles per hour in the film, after the picture was finished we took it out on a test track and had it up to 94.7 miles per hour - pretty impressive for a car that was made in our workshop.\" Late in the film, Batman employs yet another of his 'big guns.' As the Joker parades down Gotham's main street with poison gas-spewing balloons, the batwing fighter aircraft appears in the night sky. Though ninety percent of the airplane footage was done with model work, Evans and his crew were commissioned to create a full-scale version based on an Anton Furst design. \"Obviously,\" Furst noted, \"the batwing was based on the bat symbol. I looked at the bat symbol and realized that if I extended the central area - the fuselage - I would have an aircraft there. It also looked like it could be a hovercraft of some kind - almost multidirectional. So the bat symbol as an aircraft had very interesting potential, I thought, and I started working from there. It also lead to the idea of the aircraft coming out of the clouds, making a vertical climb and hitting the moon - creating the bat symbol there. I fought like hell for that shot and we just barely got it in.\" The full-scale batwing was used only for shots of the plane after it has crash-landed at the steps of the cathedral. After forming the entire shape out of wood, the effects crew reinforced it with steel and then dressed it with authentic aircraft components. \"We had a guy who went around and just raped all the nearby areas for dead aircraft parts,\" Furst stated. \"He had found an actual Harrier cockpit while I was still designing the batwing, and it was such a great find that I designed the entire cockpit based on that.\" A multilayered paint job - similar to the one done on the batmobile - was then applied to the plane. A gun metal gray color was used instead of black to ensure that the batwing could be seen in the night sky. A section representing approximately half of the entire batwing was also built to accommodate shots of Batman inside the cockpit, as well as closeups of the batwing's rockets and guns being employed. \"The partial batwing incorporated the cockpit area and another ten feet on either side,\" Evans explained. \"It was mounted on a gimbal on the stage so that we could simulate the flying motion, and we had the rockets on hydraulic rams. Even though it was only supposed to represent half of the aircraft, it was still very large - about twenty-five feet wide and thirty feet across.\" Though on a smaller scale, Batman's multipurpose utility belt had been one of Evans' earliest concerns. \"I brought up the utility belt the first time I met with Tim. I thought it was important that we make it a moving thing, rather than just a belt with gadgets hanging from it like a handyman or a marine would When the Joker initiates his plot to slaughter the inhabitants of Gotham City by emitting poison gas from giant parade balloons, Batman comes to the rescue in his batwing aircraft. As large as it was, the backlot set still extended only a few stories up. In order for the towering skyscrapers to be seen in their entirety as the batwing rocketed through the concrete canyons, Derek Meddings and his modelmakers created a massive cityscape miniature on the effects stage. Fourteen buildings ranging from twenty to thirty feet tall were aligned along a street that was forty feet long. / For economy, only the front and one side of each building was constructed. Back sides were left open so that lights could be shone through windows facing the street. / A motion control camera cruises down a line of buildings for a plate shot. CINEFEX 41 • 23"],["24","Batman","miniature - which extended nearly to the rafters of the backing. The motion control camera - built specifically lowered into the set from an positioned directly over the required a primary pass for secondary pass to enhance the practical lighting. / Only one of the Joker's giant gas balloons was constructed full-size. The others were small plastic models flown on wires and photographed against a bluescreen for later skyward via matte paintings. Erected behind the stage - was a giant sky for the project - was overhead rail system street. Most of the plates the buildings and a insertion into live-action street material extended 24 . • CINEFEX 41 wear. So I put the idea to Tim that we have the belt revolve. He liked that concept and told us to go ahead with it.\" Using a mold from the original batsuit belt designed by Bob Ringwood, Evans and his crew constructed a revolving utility belt that was chaindriven by a small electric motor in the rear. \"It was a tricky thing because the belt was not very big and some of the gadgets were quite large. The belt was supposed to have about six different gadgets on it - the ninja wheel, the grappling hook, the spear gun, the gauntlet, the batarang and the smoke pellets - but to keep the weight down, we usually had only two on the belt at any given time. The most difficult part of the whole thing was that Tim wanted all of the gadgets to actually work - they were all practical - and yet they had to be very compact to fit on the belt. The gauntlet, for example, fires out two wires which Batman then flies down, and that was all for real. That gadget actually fired those wires. So the wire strength had to be adequate for him to fly down it and yet the whole thing had to fit into a space of about seven-and-a-half inches by five inches.\" Powered by charges connected to relays, the gadgets were fired via small detonators incorporated within. Several complete utility belts were required for the film. \"We had a few dummy ones for long shots and then three fully-functional ones for the three different units that were shooting at the same time - each of which had all of the six gadgets on them. It was quite a job - and we were doing all of this during the same twelve weeks of preproduction when we were also building the batmobilel\" As physical effects supervisor, Evans was likewise responsible for all of the wire work and pyrotechnics in the film. Given Batman's propensity to fly in and out of the action on wires, the wire work was extensive but surprisingly undemanding. \"It was nothing like Superman where we had to worry about concealing the wires,\" said Evans. \"In Batman the wire is always visible, so it was just a matter of rigging it. We had him in a harness and we also put rods in the cape and shot air through it so that it would flow very nicely. The first shot of Batman floating onto the rooftop where the two thugs are hiding out was done with a counterweight rig, which was not a problem at all. We also had wire work for the scene of the Joker hanging on the ledge just before he falls into the acid. Jack Nicholson did that himself - not the fall, but he was hanging there, sixty feet in the air, on just a single wire.\" After Jack Napier's fall into the acid, the newly-born Joker calls a meeting of the city's crime bosses to proclaim his dominion over the group. One mob leader makes the unfortunate mistake of casting a dissenting vote - and is burnt to a literal crisp by the Joker's lethal version of the novelty electric handshake buzzer. \"That was done in two stages,\" noted Evans. \"In the first stage he just starts to smoke up. We had the actor piped up and we used a smoke machine to generate smoke out of his collar and behind him. We also dunked certain areas of his clothing in a chemical so that the fabric would dissolve. Then we just cut away and put in a dummy skeleton.\" Once the Joker's preeminence in Gotham's crime scene is established, he commandeers the Axis Chemical factory as his headquarters. Realized through a combination of interior sets, models and exterior shots of an abandoned power station at Acton - which had previously served as a major setting for Aliens - the Axis Chemical plant becomes a towering inferno when Batman delivers two bombs at its doorstep. Though total destruction of the factory was accomplished using a model, much of the Acton location was actually set ablaze under Evans' supervision. \"That was quite a big number for us. Tim wanted it all for real, so we pumped liquid gas into the whole place. It took ten days just to prepare the two thousand yards of steel pipe work and we had three liquid gas tankards brought in. When you are working with liquid gas you have to make sure everything is very safe - all of the joints and connections have to be sealed properly. So it was a major job. We piped the liquid gas all the pay though it way through so that the whole place went up in hundred-foot flames - you could not get anywhere near the blaze. The heat was so intense that it was actually melting and twisting nineinch girders. All of the cameras had to be shielded and the flames were visible from five miles away. The fire brigade came out every hour. We had warned them about what we were going to do, but they still had to come out because people kept reporting this huge blaze in the abandoned power plant.\" Smaller-scale explosions on the interior sets were also provided by Evans and company. Unfortunately, after the sets had been destroyed by the special effects crew, the decision was made to reshoot some of the earlier scenes in the chemical plant. \"We had to build that set twice,\" Anton Furst commented. \"They shot the sequence where Batman confronts Jack Napier - with Napier eventually falling into the acid - and then we blew the set up. But when they cut the whole sequence together they found they had not gotten a satisfactory shot of Batman flying up and out of the scene. So over Christmas we built the whole top part again. Actually it was different the second time - we redesigned it to accommodate some really inventive suggestions that Jack Nicholson had come up with. It would have been criminal not to do them, and since we were rebuilding it anyway, the producers said, 'Let's do it.\" Nicholson's suggestion for the chemical factory set entailed creating a heightened sense of vertigo for the audience. \"A stage is only so high. So to get that massive vertigo effect, we built two sets - each using the entire height of the stage. One was a specific set looking ne entire heightops t down as Jack drops to the vat of acid below and the other set was for when the camera is looking up during the shootout. You could not possibly have that height on a stage, so we built it in two parts - the looking down shots on one stage and the looking up shots on another - and then put them together.\" The physical effects effort for Batman was by far the most demanding that Burton had encountered in his short directing career - but it was also the most satisfying. \"It seems that usually you have to push effects people to go further - but John Evans starts there. When he blows up a television or something ... man, he just blows it up! And yet, i felt very safe with him. The"],["25","Batman","director is always walking this fine line with physical effects - you have to be concerned with safety and yet you want to get the shot. With John, I always got the shot and I felt entirely comfortable with what he was doing.\" Not all of the effects were accomplished full-scale and on the set. Operating a separate unit entirely was celebrated visual effects supervisor Derek Meddings whose task it was to generate all of Batman's miniature and optical effects - an assignment that would entail animation, matte paintings, motion control and extensive model work. Of primary importance were the miniatures that would complement the backlot Gotham City set Anton Furst had built in full-scale. The flawless blending of the live-action with the model photography had, from the begin- \"I never forgot what someone said ning, been a major concern. early on in the production,\" Burton recalled. \" We have these huge sets, but they represent only forty feet out of the twothousand-foot-tall skyline. It is a mere fraction of what is really supposed to be there.' The majority of Gotham City had to be created with miniatures, and that was a scary thought. If the miniatures were even a little bit wrong, we would suddenly have a completely different look. It would not seem like the same buildings or city anymore. So our major concern was always to keep the full-scale sets and the miniatures looking the same. We ran into some trouble with that, but it was more a design problem than an effects problem. For instance, I wanted a sort of vibrant night sky which we got beautifully on some of the miniature shots. But that did not link up with the live-action very well. So we were always trying to find a medium ground. We could not have the effects doing one thing and then not have the ability to shoot that same thing live-action.\" Burton's concerns notwithstanding, Meddings went into the project confident that the miniature and live-action footage could be suitably matched. \"Paul Wilson - whom I have worked with for fourteen years - is one of the best miniature cameramen in the world,\" Meddings stated. \"All he needs to do is look at the main unit setups and see where the lights are and he can then miniaturize that lighting when it comes time to do our shots. He has some very small lights that he uses and he has never failed to get it right - he just has an eye for it.\" At the request of the director, the Meddings Magic Camera company - located at Shepperton Studios - moved its base of operations to Pinewood. \"Tim really wanted the miniatures to be shot in the same studio as the live-action,\" Meddings explained, \"and for us, it was advantageous to have the director close at hand - we could walk over a couple of stages to get an okay, instead of making a half-hour drive. Also, I know Pinewood very well - I did five Bond films there and they have a really good setup for shooting miniatures. It is an overhead rigging system which allows the lights to be lowered from a grid on telescopic arms - which is fantastic for shooting miniatures because you can build a set covering the whole floor and light it without having to surround the set with lamps on stands.\" While most of the Meddings stage equipment - including a motion CINEFEX 41 • 25 NARCH THEATRE"],["26","Batman","26 * CINEFEX 41"],["27","Batman","control camera suspended from overhead rails - was either relocated or fashioned from scratch at Pinewood, the permanently fixed optical equipment remained behind at Shepperton. The film's opening shot of Gotham City was actually a combination of scenic art and miniatures. \"The opening shot of the city was done with a sixty-foot-long painted backing and miniatures. Brian Bishop did the original painting and then I went in and repainted the sky portion of it when Tim Burton decided he wanted a more dramatic looking sky. In the foreground we had a one-thirty-second scale bridge - which worked out to be about twenty feet long. On the bridge we had strings of tracer lights - the ones that run in tubes - some red and some white so that they looked like headlights and taillights of vehicles driving across the bridge. In front of that we had miniature tugboats on the water - which was actually just a black plastic. To give the impression that it was a moving river, we had two layers of black plastic and the top layer was pulled across on rollers. We also smoked up the stage to create a sense of aerial perspective. Later we used this same setup for a shot when the helicopter flies towards the city. In that instance, it was a background plate for a bluescreen composite.\" Other miniatures - built under the supervision of secondgeneration effects technician Mark Meddings - included the Axis Chemical factory which was used to complement liveaction footage of the plant's destruction. Because Tim Burton had given very specific instructions as to where and how the structure should disintegrate during the blast, the six-foot-tall model was constructed, prescored along designated lines and then lightly put back together like a jigsaw puzzle. A pyrotechnic charge was placed in a strategic position so that, when detonated, the cutaway sections of the model would blow apart predictably. Since the scene was to include a live element of the batmobile speeding away from the Acton location site, it was necessary to insure a seamless blend between the live-action and the model photography. Meddings was on hand to oversee the full-scale pyrotechnics executed by the Evans unit. \"We had to be certain that the explosions they did on the full-size set did not extend above the matte line. Since we were going to do a simple split-screen shot with our miniature footage, it was very important that the fire and smoke stay below the matte line. Immediately after they shot the bottom part of the scene, I had it put on video so that we could match our miniature with their live-action sequence. We did our explosion of the plant on stage with a sky backing - using various fire effects - and shot it at 120 frames per second.\" The most ambitious miniature attempted by the Meddings unit was a gigantic model of Gotham City's main street constructed primarily for plate shots of the batwing flying over and amongst the towering skyscrapers. \"The real buildings were supposed to be a thousand feet high,\" Meddings observed, \"so obviously we had to scale it way down. We decided on one-thirtysecond scale - which was convenient, because at that scale we were able to buy a lot of the cars we needed rather than make Batwing models were constructed in three different scales. The largest was ten feet long and was used for closeup shots of guns and rockets being fired. Motion control plates of the city would eventually replace the bluescreen backgrounds. / The smallest batwing - only six inches long - was employed for a dramatic up-angle as the craft soars over the city. / To accomplish the shot, some of the building models were tilted over at a severe angle so that the sky backing would appear to be above them. The batwing was then flown from a small wire rig. / Most of the batwing shots featured models suspended on wires and shot highspeed against miniature settings - a single-pass technique favored by Derek Meddings in most instances. An exception was a particularly complex maneuver in which the batwing hovered for a moment in front of the moon to form the familiar bat symbol. A full motion control composite was required to accomplish the feat. CINEFEX 41 • 27"],["28","Batman","Mounted on a support rod draped in black velvet, the ten-foot batwing model is readied for some aerial battle maneuvers. A radiocontrolled puppet inside the cockpit provided sufficient movement to intercut smoothly with live-action material of Michael Keaton filmed in a full-scale partial mockup of the ship. / Design inspiration for the batwing came from the bat symbol. By extending the central 'head' area into a fuselage, Anton Furst was able to elaborate upon the notion and extrapolate a fully aerodynamic craft in head and shoulder three dimensions. 28 4 CINEFEX 41 them. The cars would never be identified in the sequence, so it did not really matter what they were. All we had to do was paint them. Of course, all the street signs and all the lamp stands and all the street dressings had to be made in this very minute scale. Also there was a whole lot of detail on the buildings which was difficult to deal with. The modelmakers built specific sections and then we made molds so we could very quickly produce large pieces of the buildings and then just stick them all together on a wooden framework. To save money, we built only two sides of the buildings - the front and one side. So when we did a reverse shot - as in some scenes where the batwing is coming toward the camera - we had to turn all the buildings around the other way. Even though we were looking at the same thing again, it was just different enough so that nobody seemed to notice - even the director.\" While buildings on the Gotham City backlot set were replicated in detail and extrapolated skyward, it was also necessary for the model unit to produce additional structures so the street could be extended for the batwing's flight. When completed, the cityscape consisted of fourteen buildings ranging from twenty to thirty feet tall on a street that was forty feet long. Though the model was unusually large, the claustrophobic nature of the city as designed by Anton Furst made for miniature streets that were decidedly narrow. \"We could not walk between the buildings,\" Meddings noted. 'We could just barely squeeze by them sideways. And because the streets were so narrow, we could not use the motion control rig we had at Shepperton to shoot our POVs of the batwing flying through. That system was just too big. So Peter Talbot - one of my cameramen - designed a motion control rig especially for those shots. We used a Mitchell camera with a 16mm lens so that we could get the depth of focus required - sometimes we were only shooting at three frames per second. The rig itself had to be built so that it would not protrude on either side of the camera too much. It was mounted on overhead tracks and powered hydraulically so that it could raise and lower depending on what kind of movement was needed for the plate. We had to build and install the whole system - including the overhead tracks. Even though this motion control setup was used strictly for POV shots, we still had to shoot two passes. We did one pass on the model city and then went in and did a second pass to enhance the practical lighting. We needed to get a certain amount of brilliance out of the lights and we also had to oil the street in order to get reflections that would 1a in the street match up with the live-action shots.' A larger-scale miniature set was required for high-speed shots of the batwing being downed by the Joker and crash-landing onto the cathedral steps. Rather than recreate the minute building details all the way down to street level, Meddings took advantage of the fact that the lower portions of the buildings had already been constructed in full-scale on the backlot. \"We took photographs of the full-scale buildings, then reduced them down to the scale that we needed and used them as backlit transparencies in the windows to save us from building a lot of tiny interiors. We had to build about six buildings and part of the big cathedral in twelfth-scale for that sequence - also a twelfth-scale batwing. The main unit had already shot the wreckage of the batwing on the cathedral steps and the problem for us was that the plane had to land in exactly the same place and position in order to match what they had photographed. We flew our model on wires and shot it at 120 frames per second with six cameras on it. On the first try we got it onto the steps, but it was not positioned exactly right. So we have a very clever cut there and just did the last part of the batwing hitting the steps in the correct position. That is followed by an explosion and then the scene cuts to the main unit work.\" Complicating the crash sequence was the fact that the effects unit had only a single twelfth-scale batwing to shoot. \"We only had time to build one and that was a bit of a worry because we knew we would have to be very lucky to get the shot on the first take. Of course, we did not get it on the first take. And since the model had charges on it that went off as it touched down, we had really wrecked a lot of the batwing in our first attempt at the shot. So we had to rebuild it in one day and, luckily, on the second take with the six cameras we got what we thought we needed. Still we had to cover it more, so we repositioned the cameras and just stuck all the pieces of the model back on - makeshit s really a makeshift job. Fortunately the batwing comes in so fast - streaming smoke and everything - that no one ever notices there are bits and pieces missing from it.\" In addition to the principal twelfth-scale model, two other versions of the batwing were constructed. \"We built a quarterscale batwing - about ten feet long - that was used for all of the closeup shots of guns and rockets coming out. Inside it was a radio-controlled Batman puppet for scenes of him flying. That version of the batwing was shot against a bluescreen and composited into some of the motion control shots we did on the street set. Then we had a six-inch model - one-forty-eighthscale - that was used for one particular long shot of the plane flying behind a building. It was from Vicki's point of view, and everyone is looking up and all of the buildings are in perspective against the sky with this very miniature batwing flying behind them. We did all of that in one shot.\" Except for a few special shots which required motion control composites, all of the batwing footage was accomplished in single passes using the overhead wire rig. \"Because we were shooting at 120 frames per second, we had to have a rig that could propel the models very fast. We built one that was on a very high-speed motor. It was an 'endless' wire rig - a simple setup with the wire in a loop around pulleys and the batwing connected to it. The model would shoot down the loop. Then when we wanted the batwing to repeat its move, we just reversed the motor and it went back up the wire. It was essential - especially for the crash sequence - that we have a high-speed motor on the rig so that the batwing could travel down the street at the necessary speed. As it was, the batwing was traveling so fast that it was not until we saw rushes the next day that we could really"],["29","Batman","even see what had happened. We had videos hooked up to all the cameras and we could run them at a simulated 120 frames per second, but it was not like watching it in the cinema.\" Even a complex shot of the batwing snagging the poison gas balloons was done with a wire rig. \"The batwing collects the balloons and then flies around the cathedral. Both the batwing and the plastic balloons were flown on wires. The balloons were about a foot in length - in scale with the batwing model we were using. for the background, we took our street set model of the top of the cathedral and put it in front of the painted backing of the city we had used for the opening scene. Then, behind the cathedral model, we had all of the balloons connected to the batwing and flew the whole thing on a pole arm. There was just the one rig holding both the batwing and the balloons and we flew it in sort of an arc around the back of the cathedral.\" treati me A shot of the batwing hovering momentarily against the moon - the scene Anton Furst had fought to get into the film - was one of the few motion control composites in the sequence. Others included shots where the batwing maneuvers were too complicated to perform on a wire rig. \"In one instance, the batwing had to come in from left to right, turn, climb, bank and then go back out the way it came in. It would take a very complicated wire system to do all that - in fact we tried to do that and it did not work. So we had to go with motion control for a few shots, even though I much prefer to do flying shots with wires if it is humanly possible. I think you get a freer look with wires - there is that human element when you have someone flying the model on the end of a pole arm. And motion control takes time - you have to shoot the background and then the whole thing has to be put together optically. I like to go to rushes the next day and see the completed shot and know that another one can be crossed off the list.\" After the batwing crashes, the Joker drags Vicki to the top of the cathedral bell tower to await the helicopter in which he will make his escape. Shaken, but not seriously injured, Batman climbs from the wreckage of his craft and gives pursuit. Although a thirty-foot-tall cathedral model had been included in the thirty-second-scale main street miniature, the climactic bell tower sequence required a separate sixty-foot-tall model. \"The thirty-foot model was just not large enough to accommodate the end sequence,\" Meddings explained. \"Shots of Batman and Vicki hanging onto the gargoyle with the helicopter approaching had to be done on a larger scale because we had these very small figures of Batman and Vicki and the Joker. We built it in three sections to accommodate different parts of the sequence. The model was so tall that we had to shoot most of it on its side. For a shot of the helicopter approaching the top of the cathedral with the characters standing there watching, we dismantled the lower part of the model and just shot the top section.\" A separate model of the bell tower was constructed for interior shots of the massive bell crashing through floor after floor until it strikes the bottom. The seemingly endless staircase inside the tower was actually a forty-foot-tall miniature, but even CINFFEX 41 • 29"],["30","Batman","30 - CINEFEX 41"],["31","Batman","that was considered too short. A simple trick effectively doubled its apparent height. \"For the bottom we took a still photograph of the miniature and had it reduced, then laid it on the floor so that we could continue the shot as far as possible - it looked like the staircase just went down forever. Obviously, when the bell hit the photograph there had to be a cut. We built the staircase out of balsa wood so that it would break easily and we actually dropped a brass bell that was about ten inches in diameter. We had it on a quick release and we lined it up so that it would wreck the staircase as it went down. To get another perspective, we cut a hole in the side of the model and put a second camera in there, looking up. I had a friendly argument with my cameraman who was concerned that if the bell hit the staircase and bounced and hit the camera that it would mean the total writeoff of our very expensive lens. There was really no way we could protect it; but because the bell was heavy, I did not think there was any way that it could hit a balsa wood structure and bounce off. Fortunately, I was right.\" Since local authorities would not permit helicopters to fly in the area of the studio at night, an alternative had to be found. \"The helicopter could not be flown because we were too close to Heathrow Airport,\" Meddings explained. \"It is very difficult to get permission for helicopters to fly around the airport area - especially at night. Flying a real helicopter would not have been that effective anyway, because it was winter and there would have been a totally black sky behind it. Tim did not want that — he wanted to see some kind of cloud formation.\" For live-action shots showing the Joker's henchmen approaching the cathedral rooftop, an actual helicopter was mounted on a fixed platform on the stage - with the camera creating the hovering movement. Miniature helicopters were also built for the sequence. \"Our miniature helicopter was in the same scale as the cathedral model - about a foot long - and was flown on wires for long shots of the approach. We also built a five-foot model of the helicopter for closer shots. It was built as a radiocontrolled model, but we did not radio-control it because that would have been a real problem on the stage. And we could not fly it outside because we wanted to control what the sky looked like in the shots. So the five-foot model was also just flown on wires using an overhead rig. Because some of the shots were at an extreme angle looking up at the helicopter, we flew it on its side and shot it at about a forty-five-degree angle. By doing that, we were able to avoid the problem of having to hang a backing at the top of the stage, which is always difficult.\" In addition to the predominant building models, the Meddings unit also contributed various smaller models to be used for insert shots - including a two-foot-long miniature batmobile that was ultimately never used. \"We thought we would be able to do things with a radio-controlled model that the real batmobile could not do,\" Meddings related, \"but the real one turned out to be very maneuverable, so all of the scenes with the batmobile were done live-action. The only sequence we were involved with was the one in which the batmobile is approachFor scenes of the batwing crash-landing in the street, the Meddings unit built another expansive city miniature at a larger scale than the principal cityscape constructed earlier for the plate photography. Six buildings and part of the cathedral were required. / To avoid having to build detailed interiors for the street-level shops, backlit photo transparencies of corresponding storefronts on the full-scale set were inserted into the miniature display windows. Prior to photography, the streets were sprayed with oil to duplicate the wet look captured during the liveaction shoot. / Effects crew members prepare the batwing for its wire-rigged descent. / Six high-speed cameras documented the impact - which was pyrotechnically enhanced. / Live-action scenes employed a full-scale batwing constructed by John Evans and his crew. CINEFEX 41 • 31"],["32","Batman","The full-size batwing was used solely for post-crash scenes on the cathedral steps. Bottled gas was fed into various sections of the mockup and ignited when needed during takes. The complicated the miniature crash photography because Meddings and his crew had to insure that their wirerigged model ended up in precisely the same attitude. / For scenes in which the Joker's goons try to airlift closeups of the actors were shot in a full-size helicopter that was mounted on a fixed miniature buildings and wire-flown helicopters in fact that the live-action material was shot first their boss from the cathedral bell tower, platform. Hovering movement was created solely with the camera. Wider shots employed two different scales. 32 * CINEFEX 41 ing the rock face of the cliff and then goes into the tunnel towards the batcave. Since it was a POV from inside the vehicle, we did not need to use a miniature car, but we built the rock face and the tunnel in miniature to get the plates.\" As the batmobile races towards the cliff, it appears to go right through the wall. \"That was actually very simple. We tried to think of all kinds of clever ways to do it with holograms and such, but in the end we found that if we just raced the camera right up to the rock until it hit - with a guard on the front of the camera to prevent it from being damaged - and then cut immediately to inside the tunnel, it worked very well. So it was just a straightforward cut - with maybe one or two black frames - and then the car is in the tunnel. We had lights on the front of the camera to simulate the headlights of the batmobile. Then when we did the reverse shot, we had fading red lights on the back of the rig so that we could get the red glow of the taillights. It added a bit of realism that helped the shot.\" For the sequence in which Batman and Vicki - trapped in an alleyway - escape the Joker's goons by flying up a wire to the gantry above, the Meddings crew constructed a miniature alleyway. \"A shot was done on the stage where the actors were actually lifted off the floor, but the longer shot of the two of them being taken up to the gantry was done with miniatures. We built little three-inch-tall Vicki and Batman puppets with loosejointed legs so that as they were pulled up the wire their legs moved convincingly.\" While the extensive model work constituted the largest part of his Batman assignment, Meddings also provided approximately ninety bluescreen opticals. \"For scenes where Batman and Vicki fall from the cathedral rooftop, we shot the actors against bluescreen. That material was then composited against shots of some very detailed sections of the cathedral that we built specifically for that purpose. Scenes where they come in at the top of the frame and fall out at the bottom were quite simple - all were done with just a camera move. For shots of them falling away from the camera, the actors were hung on their sides and the camera tracked backwards. When it had gone back as far as it could and reduced them to as small as possible, we took over with an optical zoom and reduce them even more.\" Bluescreen was also used for some key shots during the Joker's macabre balloon parade down Gotham City's main street. \"We built miniature plastic balloons and flew them on wires,\" Meddings explained. \"Only one full-size balloon was made for that particular sequence. There was no point in making all of the balloons full-size since they could not be used on the main unit set - the buildings only went up forty feet. If real balloons had been used, they would have been floating against a black sky and not against buildings. We put the main unit balloon footage on videotape and I matched the miniature balloons by lining them up against the video. The bottom part of the scene was live-action and the top part with the continuation of the buildings was a matte painting. The balloons were just bluescreened into the shot.\" Most of the one hundred and sixty opticals employed in the film went into the parade sequence. \"When the Joker's floats are coming down the street, there are searchlights playing all over the buildings. All of those had to be put in afterwards because the live-action was shot at night in the middle of winter and there was always a slight mist in the air. If real searchlights had been used on the set and tilted up through that mist they would have gone through the matte lines. There was no way we could match a white searchlight beam with an animated one. So for a lot of those shots we did not actually have the lights on - the units just moved around as if they were shining on the buildings and then we put in the searchlight beam with animation.\" Peter Chiang was responsible for all of the animation effects. Included among them was the very first glimpse of Batman in the film. As two thugs sit on a rooftop counting the money they just confiscated from some hapless tourists, a batlike shadow is seen crossing an adjoining rooftop. \"We had originally tried to get that shot with a miniature Batman,\" Meddings recalled, \"but it was too small and it did not work very well. The shot always worried me because there was so much going on in the frame area that I did not think anyone would notice Batman walking around up there. So Peter did it with cel animation with the idea of making it more noticeable.\" On a flashier note, Chiang also provided the batmobile with its steel plate shielding. \"The batmobile was shot on the main unit set. Then we had registration prints made and used those to do cel animation. We also brought the batmobile onto the stage and photographed large areas of it so that we could get the right texture on our cutting shots. We retouched the still photographs and used them for animating the shields. That way we could match the lighting. When the shields came over the batmobile, the lighting was exactly the same as on the full-size car.\" The final shot of the film - depicting Batman in silhouette in the hi sky e against the night sky - employed a combination of animation effects, miniatures and bluescreen. \"The camera tracks up the buildings to reveal Batman standing against the sky. We used a couple of our miniature buildings, put them on their sides and then tracked the camera along the length of them, dissolving through two different buildings and then finally going into the live-action shot of Batman. Michael Keaton was photographed standing against a bluescreen backing and later we put the sky in and the bat sign coming up in the clouds with animation. Frankly, that was not my favorite shot. I felt there should have been more of a swirling effect if the bat symbol was actually being projected onto clouds.\" Matte paintings created by Ray Capel and Leigh Took provided various establishing shots of Gotham City, as well as most of the interior of the batcave. Shots in the batcave involving actors were done by photographing the live-action and then combining it optically with the matte paintings. \"We projected the live-action up onto glass which was painted white,\" Meddings explained. \"Then we drew it out and did the rest of the painting and finally put the two together. Other shots where the"],["33","Batman","matte paintings had to have searchlights and smoke on them were done with supplemental animation. We also photographed some searchlights off of very bright follow spots against black velvet and just double-exposed those into the actual matte shot. The smoke was done the same way - we shot it against black velvet and then double-exposed that over the actual shot.\" Even with five months to complete the opticals - following a month of preparation and four months of shooting - the visual effects schedule proved much too tight for peace of mind. But up-tothe-last-minute work by Meddings and his crew ensured that the film met its June 1989 release date. \"The challenge with effects,\" noted Burton, \"is that there is never enough time. You come to a point where the time runs out and you just have to call it quits. Well, we're releasing the film tomorrow. I guess we have to stop. It gets to the point frustration of effects is that you never know what is going to work and what is not. Some of the stuff that I thought would not work at all worked the best.\" Moving from the arena of moderate-budget films destined for cult status to the pressure and hoopla of big-budget filmmaking was a definite learning experience for the young director. \"I realized that when you get into a movie with this kind of budget and on this scale, people do not want to see the kinds of effects that I had on Beetlejuice or Pee Wee's Big Adventure. A lot of the concepts I had for the film were very much me - I am probtike a that on ably one of the few people who actually like the idea that the batwing gets shot down by the Joker's pistol. I just enjoy that kind of thing - the irony of it. But irony gets lost in these blockbuster-mentality films. I have a very specific attitude about effects and what I want to see. I like a certain lack of slickness - which sometimes gets me into trouble. I have to be careful about my own concept of 'lack of slickness' - because by 'lack of slickness,' I do not mean bad. It's rather ethereal. The concept works on Beetlejuice because that film is perceived as a lowish-budget kind of film. Batman is not perceived as that, and yet the effects still follow my personal philosophy.\" It was a personal philosophy that helped to create the blockbuster movie of a blockbuster summer. Batman fever gripped the country - no doubt due, in part, to one of the most successful and widespread marketing campaigns ever conceived to launch a motion picture. Early in the film, the Joker laments to one of his lackeys: \"What kind of world do we live in where a man dressed up as a bat gets all my press?\" Before the summer was over, the makers of the films that had gone up against Batman were left pondering the same question. Batman photographs courtesy Warner Brothers. TMs and ® 1989 DC Comics Inc. All rights reserved. Production unit still photography by Murray Close, Keith Hamshere and Douglas Luke. Effects unit still photography by Neil Sharp. Special thanks to Jeff Walker and Jess Garcia. CINEFEX 41 • 33"],["34","Honey, I Shrunk the Kids",""],["35","Honey, I Shrunk the Kids","backyard odyssey article by Susan Dayton"],["36","Honey, I Shrunk the Kids","Four small-town children are reduced to quarter-inch shrinking machine tinkered fathers goes temporarily berserk in Honey, I Shrunk familiar backyard in which filled with everyday objects scale. With big aspirations director Joe Johnston and executive producer Thomas G. Smith - both veterans of Industrial Light and Magic - who assembled a top-notch crew of independent effects artists to create a magical proportions. Johnston works with one of his young actors - Thomas Brown - on the Churubusco in Mexico City. / Studio craftsmen construct a giant key which the kids charge of the large-scale sets and props was production size when a prototype together by one of their the Kids. Suddenly the they find themselves becomes a dense jungle and creatures rendered terrifying by the shift in and a small budget, the project was helmed by world of colossal backyard set at Estudios find lost in the yard. In designer Gregg Fonseca. 36 • CINEFEX 41 After redefining the horror film with massive infusions of grand guinol excess in Re-Animatorand From Beyond, director Stuart Gordon and producer Brian Yuzna were ready for something different in their next collaboration. Though the mad scientist' motif would be retained - at least in its broadest sense - the setting would be shifted from bloody operating rooms and morgues to a quiet small-town neighborhood where a group of ordinary children undergo an extraordinary adventure. Headless walking corpses and supernatural beings would be put in mothballs and replaced by commonplace ants and scorpions and honeybees - tiny creatures made fearsome adversaries by the fact that the children have been inadvertently shrunken to a quarter-of-an-inch in size by an invention tinkered together by one of their fathers. Lost in a grassy backyard which now seems like a towering jungle to them, the youngsters must find their way home and pray for restoration. It was with no small irony that the grand masters of graphic gore found a home for their fairy tale project at Walt Disney Pictures - the acknowledged epicenter of family entertainment. Assigned by Disney to executive produce the film was Thomas G. Smith. As general manager of Industrial Light and Magic, Smith had guided the celebrated effects facility through a succession of award-winning excursions into the varied worlds of Star Wars and Star Trek and Indiana Jones - also Poltergeist and E.T. But after eight years with Lucasfilm, Smith had left to pursue career aspirations as an independent producer. His initial involvement with Disney came when he was engaged to do postproduction on the Captain Eo 3-D extravaganza for the studio theme parks. Now - with his solid background in effects - Smith was considered an ideal candidate to oversee what would eventually come to be known as Honey, I Shrunk the Kids. \"We were given approximately $20,000 to do a test that would show Disney whether we could produce an effects movie on a low budget,\" Smith recalled. \"The nature of the film required a large number of special effects and we had to do them the most inexpensive way possible - otherwise the film would not be made at all. At the time, the effects budget was only $2 million.\" Illusion Arts - a small facility headed by Bill Taylor and Syd Dutton - was contracted to produce four effects shots for the test. Three employed traditional bluescreen opticals to miniaturize children and place them within giant-sized environments. A fourth featured a young girl riding on the back of an ant - a scene created by compositing a real child onto a stopmotion puppet. When completed, the tests were presented to Disney and enthusiastically received. \"The four shots convinced Disney that a number of effects could indeed be done on a very small budget - but we were still concerned about the fate of the movie. The budget at that time was so small that it appeared we still might not be able to do it. Rather than having the luxury of hiring one special effects house to produce the effects for the entire production, we had to do it ourselves using the little boutique houses - places like Illusion Arts, Hollywood Optical and Visual Concept Engineering. We also used people like Dave Allen and Phil Tippett - talented people who, over the years, have decided they prefer the freedom of working for themselves rather than in a large establishment. By using the smaller houses, we were able to keep the cost considerably below what trza we might have paid had we gone to one of the bigger effects facilities. I was also given a sense of control I might not otherwise have had. From an aesthetic point of view, it meant that the shots had to be extremely well designed so we could get away with using traditional and fairly simple techniques such as bluescreen, rear projection, rotoscoping and miniature sets.\" In the spring of 1987, the production set up shop at Estudios Churubusco in Mexico City. Churubusco was chosen not only for its eight giant soundstages, but because the cost of producing the movie in Mexico would be a fraction of what it would cost - even at a smaller studio - in the United States. Not since Dune had Churubusco seen the sort of large-scale sets required for Honey, I Shrunk the Kids. With a core group of American technicians - augmented by skilled carpenters, electricians and laborers hired in Mexico - the production commenced with Stuart Gordon at the helm. It was an abortive start, however. \"After six months on the project,\" said Smith, \"Stuart became ill and could not continue. Once again we thought the film was doomed. The special effects had been completely storyboarded. Our backyard sets had been built and we were in the process of building the interiors. Then suddenly we lost our director - also our producer. Brian Yuzna and Stuart Gordon were partners - and when Stuart left the production, Brian backed out also.\" Engaging a new producer was not a problem. Penney Finkelman Cox - coproducer on Terms of Endearment and Broadcast News - stepped effortlessly into the post. Finding a new director was a somewhat thornier proposition. While at ILM, Smith had worked closely with Joe Johnston on a number of highprofile productions. As senior art director at the facility, Johnston had designed distinctive vehicles and characters for all three of the Star Wars films and contributed extensively to other projects as well. Smith was well aware that the success of his current venture would be largely dependent upon the believability of its visual illusions. A director was needed who could take charge of those effects. Though Johnston's directing experience had been limited to second unit work on Willow, Smith felt confident that he could handle the full range of directorial duties on Honey, I Shrunk the Kids.\"I had just returned from England after Willow,\" Johnston recalled, \"when Tom called me up and asked if I would be interested. After reading the script, I met with Jeffrey Katzenberg, chairman of the Disney motion picture division. I told him that I thought there were too many characters and too many effects. It was not a story about people, but rather a story about ants and bees and floods. Effects-wise, it went way over the top. I believe you should always leave an audience wanting to see more - and the script I read did not take that philosophy at all.\" Johnston left the meeting convinced that he would never hear from Disney again - but a second call a couple of days later brought with it an invitation to direct the"],["37","Honey, I Shrunk the Kids","film and restructure the script to his liking. A succession of writers was brought in to rework the screenplay. One of the first things to go was one of the characters. \"Originally there were five kids in the story,\" recalled Johnston, \"and there was a set of twins that had already been cast. Going from four characters to five does not make a film twenty percent more difficult, it makes it fifty percent more difficult. For every extra kid, there would be another closeup to shoot and another set of relationships to deal with. We could do without that.\" Effects sequences also came under the ax. When the final draft was completed - twelve weeks before principal photography was to begin - the number of visual effects from the original script had been cut in half. Two principal players - production designer Gregg Fonseca and mechanical effects supervisor Peter Chesney - had been brought on board during the Stuart Gordon period and were already deeply involved in manufacturing the oversize sets and props that would be needed for principal photography. With credits on such films as A Nightmare on Elm Street and House, Fonseca was well versed in stretching budgets for maximum impact. Honey, I Shrunk the Kids would be his biggest assignment - quite literally. At the top of his set list was a giant backyard with blades of grass towering forty feet into the air. \"No one had ever built a giant lawn before,\" said Fonseca. \"Even though we had a plan of attack, the process had to be very experimental. If you look closely at a lawn under the scale we were working with, you will see that everything sparkles and shines with a wonderful kind of magical quality. It was my job to duplicate that look in giant scale.\" Everything from dirt to rocks to grass was studied in minute detail. \"Soil is actually very granular and made up of a number of sizes of particles. We translated these particles into about ten thousand crystalline shapes that were made from blow molds - the same process used in making plastic soft drink containers - and scattered them all over the place. Whenever we made a discovery like this, we would play it to the hilt in an attempt to make the lawn as magical as possible. What we wanted was something that looked like an enchanted forest from one of the old Disney animated movies.\" Various kinds of grasses were purchased and examined under a microscope to determine which might serve best as a model for enlargement. \"The stalks were made of welded rebar wrapped with a corrugated cardboard similar to green fiberglass roofing material, Fonseca continued. \"Those were then sprayed with foam to form the stem of the stalk. The rebar afforded them a tremendous amount of flex. Only one mold was used for all the blades - which were cast from soft urethane foam. The imperfections in the manufacturing process - along with subtle differences in paint - tended to make each blade look different.\" The billions of tiny hairs covering real blades of grass were faithfully reproduced to scale with a technique created by lead sculptor Daniel Miller. \"Each hair was a single fiber optic cable heated in cooking oil so that it would not stick to other cables when semi-melted. The cables were heated, and then the ends were CINEFEX 41 • 37"],["38","Honey, I Shrunk the Kids","38 - CINEFEX 41"],["39","Honey, I Shrunk the Kids","pulled and stretched to produce a tapered tip that defied gravity. The technique worked beautifully, The main problem was poking all those little suckers into our foam grass blades - which required countless hours of work. At its peak, the art department employed more than two hundred and fifty Mexican craftspeople - with more than a hundred consistently at work throughout the production. Everyone from the coordinator to the painters worked furiously to put all those little cables in by hand. It took four weeks to install a total of four-and-a-half milThe finished blades became the principal fixtures of a backyard set - seventy by ninety feet - constructed on one of the largest stages at Churubusco. \"When I got down and looked at a real yard up close, I realized that there were great changes in levels. After all, three-quarters-of-and-inch in real life was equal to fifteen feet in our scale. To match these changes in level, the terrain of the giant set was also constructed irregularly. The skeleton of the set was made out of wood and the irregular shapes were covered with chicken wire and paper. An outer layer of concrete was then added because the surface had to support a tremendous amount of traffic over a long period of time.\" Originally, it had been planned to shoot exteriors for the Szalinski and Thompson houses on location. \"Stuart Gordon had found two houses in San Diego to do the exteriors around,\" said Johnston. \"One was shabby and run-down and the other had been restored. Unfortunately, we could not shoot beyond the houses because stucco condominiums had been built all over the area and what we wanted were scenes of an old-fashioned small-town neighborhood.\" The dilemma was resolved with the decision to build the entire set at Churubusco. \"The neighborhood was larger and more costly than the lawn sets,\" stated Fonseca. \"We built seven Victorian houses - the entire street - in what used to be a parking lot behind the studio buildings. Almost everything was handcrafted. The spindles for the stairways, the decorative moldings, the spires and vergeboards at the top of each house - even the legs on the kitchen table - were produced specifically for the movie because those things were not sold in Mexico City. The idea behind building a huge studio backlot set was to represent a real American neighborhood in a fictionalized Disney world. I am most proud of that, because in the reality of the movie I think the neighborhood really works.\" \"The problem with building such a set on a studio backlot.\" noted Johnston, \"is that there is usually something about it that is just too perfect - the curbs are too straight or the lawns too well-kept. The reality of it is that sidewalks are cracked, fire hydrants are always leaning - everything is slightly twisted in real life. Gregg Fonseca is great at that - making a set have the quirkiness of reality.\" Though the Szalinski and Thompson homes were built as complete structures, the remaining houses were simply facades. \"An additional house was built on wheels. Whenever we were shooting between the Szalinski and Thompson houses, we could roll it across the set to have a house across Inventor Wayne Szalinski (Rick Moranis) trains his shrinking cannon on an apple he has selected as a test subject. The device - constructed by physical effects supervisor Peter Chesney and his Image Engineering organization - featured various articulation mechanisms and a practical targeting laser built into the muzzle. To enhance the lowpower laser effect, a celanimated energy beam was inserted optically by Peter Kuran of Visual Concept Engineering. / When a mishit baseball triggers the cannon, youngsters Nick Szalinski (Robert Oliveri) and Ron Thompson (Jared Rushton) are shrunk to miniscule size as they enter the attic lab to investigate. / Their older siblings - Russ Thompson (Thomas Brown) and Amy Szalinski (Amy O'Neill) share the same fate moments later. The shrinking effect employed bluescreen photography of the actors - using both camera and optical zooms to reduce them in frame - composited into a motion control background plate. The full effect lasted less than a second. CINEFEX 41 • 39"],["40","Honey, I Shrunk the Kids","was constructed in forced perspective and dressed with dirt and dust - also a giant dead fly created by creature supervisor David Sosalla. / An enlarged dustpan section unwitting Wayne Szalinski and deposited into a trash sweeping sequence included a thousand-pound broom attached to a swinging steel framework suspended above the set. / Though the giant broom served well in tight shots, longer views required eventual compositing into broom sweeping the attic attached to the broom rig with wire safety harnesses. A large-scale attic floor was also built by Gregg Fonseca and his crew for scenes in which the tiny kids are swept up by the bag. / Props for the section made by Image Engineering from footdiameter foam cylinders photographed against a frontlit bluescreen for plate material of a real floor. / Joe Johnston performers who are that the actors be directs his young 40 • CINEFEX 41 the street.\" Principal photography began in January 1988. \"We spent the first eight weeks filming the scenes before and after the kids were shrunk,\" Johnston related. \"The idea was to shoot the parents during the first half of the schedule so that the adult actors could go home. Hiro Narita - our director of photography - set the visual tone of the movie. He has a beautiful style and he wanted the movie to look absolutely real. If it was not a believable neighborhood with real kids and real parents, then no one in the audience would believe the fantasy world after the kids are shrunk.\" Working in tandem with Narita was effects director of photography Rick Fichter whose job it was to duplicate the main unit look on the effects photography and plate work. Another ILM alumnus who had gone independent, Fichter had his hands full dealing with the huge volume of work and an essentially inexperienced crew. \"Because of our extremely low budget, Disney wanted me to hire a mostly Mexican effects crew. I flew down to Mexico and interviewed some good people - but few of them had any experience in effects. I found that being a good live-action assistant does not necessarily make one a good effects assistant. Everything had to be explained step by step. I could speak reasonable Spanish, but it needed some brushing up - so communication was tough.\" As had many American film companies over the years, the producers of Honey, I Shrunk the Kids found the economic advantages of shooting in Mexico offset considerably by the inherent nature of the place. \"Sickness was a major problem,\" said Smith. \"Nearly everybody on the show got wiped out at one point or another. In a city where the air is known to contain dust with bacteria in it, you do not even need to eat a bad taco to get sick - all you need to do is take a deep breath at the wrong time.\" The climate was also less than ideal - ranging from tropical monsoons early in the production to blazing smog-choked days near the end. In the attic of the Szalinski house is the makeshift laboratory where closet inventor Wayne Szalinski (Rick Moranis) labors day and night to perfect a shrinking machine he predicts will solve the problems of the industrial future. Essential to the plot, the device went through a protracted evolutionary process. An early rendering of the shrinking cannon had been completed by Gregg Fonseca for then-director Stuart Gordon. \"The design was based on a moving arm available commercially that we could have purchased and attached to the top of the machine,\" said Fonseca. \"Stuart felt it had too many axes of movement. My argument was that it needed the additional movement to give it a sense of personality. When Stuart left the project, we reverted to my original idea - although the commercial arm was never actually purchased. Instead, Peter Chesney and his people constructed a robotic arm specifically for the gun in the movie.\" Johnston wanted the design to reflect its creator. \"I did not want the gun to just sit there and spark - it needed to have a 643 JIL"],["41","Honey, I Shrunk the Kids","CINEFEX 41 • 41"],["42","Honey, I Shrunk the Kids","personality of its own. Since it was to be activated by motors, when it went out of control I wanted it to have the ability to search for a target or come to attention.\" Johnston solicited input from Peter Chesney in developing a final concept. As head of Image Engineering, Chesney had provided physical effects and mechanical artifacts for films ranging from The Osterman Weekendand Raising Arizona to A Nightmare on Elm Street III and House II. Though the plan had been to produce most of the technically demanding effects rigs at the company's Burbank facility, it had soon become evident that much of the work would need to be accomplished in Mexico. After a preliminary scout of the area, Chesney had decided to move his entire shop to Churubusco. \"We brought down a forty-foot container full of equipment which included a mill, welders, a lathe - even my computer. When we arrived, we were a complete manufacturing facility. All we needed was electricity.\" The cannon design went through what Chesney termed an artistic 'anti-engineering' phase. \"After Joe came on board, the tone of the film changed. The original shrinking cannon built from surplus yard parts was now an invention that a scientist might have subcontracted to machine shops - except that he assembled it in his attic and worked on it himself. We built a lifting arm based on the geometry of a parallelogram, but used cross-coupled chains and counter-rotating gears instead of hard linkage to lift the fifty-pound gun body from below. It was definitely not how you would normally engineer something of that size to go up and down and rotate and tilt, but we were trying to give it a mechanized kind of character with an open architecture of moving parts - all of which had real functions to perform. The whole idea was to make it visually interesting, At one point we were building aluminum trusses in our shop in Burbank and noticed that our aluminum welder - unlike ordinary welders - produced a beautiful blue light with unusual white sparks. So we decided to incorporate it. We redressed the wire-weld head - which looked bizarre to begin with - painted it white and rebuilt the nozzle to make it look less recognizable without affecting its performance. Then I taught Rick Moranis how to wire-weld a little - enough to reliably create showers of sparks on cue. We put an aluminum target plate on the gun so that during the takes Rick could lay down weld beads. After long takes, I would go in and pour half a quart of water over everything to cool things down and then replace the target plates. The temperature would get up to about six thousand degrees and there would be molten aluminum starting to rain down through the gear train.\" To complete the film logic of the electromagnetic gun motif, Johnston agreed that it should have a superconductor in it and that when it reached full power it would be fog-shrouded and frozen. \"We ran stainless steel webbed tubing into the gun and pumped in real liquid nitrogen. Liquid nitrogen vaporizes when it touches anything warm, so we coiled the pipes cosmetically in two places to make the vapor appear to exhaust from the bottom of the gun. The pipes were so chilled from the nitrogen that 42 - CINEFEX 41"],["43","Honey, I Shrunk the Kids","there would be a beautiful little fog falling. We had a huge liquid nitrogen tank below the floor to provide an unlimited fuel supply and then a small one actually on the set as the prop bottle which Rick Moranis could use to turn the valve on. After the nitrogen was pumped in, we had to get the temperature of the stainless steel tubing to go from seventy above to two-hundredand-fifty degrees below zero before any vapor would become visible at the end of the coils. This chill-down of the pipes created a huge whistling roar as loud as a jet airplane. The whole cooldown and whistle-out sequence took about sixty seconds. By the end of the scene, the coils would be warming up and needed to cool again - but it was such a great detail that everyone was quite happy to put up with it.\" In addition to its various articulations, the shrinking cannon also had eyes of a sort. Chesney mounted a live video camera with a monitor on top so that the object of the gun's attention could readily be seen on camera. A periscope was attached to the cannon's muzzle and used as an aiming device for its targeting laser. \"We ran a real laser across the gun through internal mirrors and out the muzzle. Since we were using only a ten milliwatt unit - which is a very lightweight laser - the fog made by the liquid nitrogen helped to accentuate the red beam.\" The laser served primarily as a visual reference for the later insertion of a more elaborate beam effect provided via cel animation by Peter Kuran of Visual Concept Engineering, \"The real laser beam was actually seen in only one shot,\" revealed Kuran, \"because a certain amount of smoke had been localized around the vicinity of the gun. In the rest of the shots the attic was fairly clear of smoke and debris, so we animated an enhanced beam into the shots to make it visually stronger. We also supplied some additional effects which surround the beam.\" With his shrinking cannon now ready for a trial run, Szalinski aims the device at its first subject - an apple. Glittering blue glowing beam strikes the apple. Jiggling and dancing uncontrollably around the table, the apple finally explodes in a burst of applesauce. The shrinking cannon has failed its test - miserably. \"We went through an entire procedure of building fake apples cast in wax and painting them to look like the real thing,\" explained Chesney. \"Then we discovered that what worked best were real apples which we hollowed out and loaded with an explosive and applesauce. For the movement of the apple, we built -that ad a into another real apple a cylinder-shaped device that had a motor and an offset weight with a radio-controlled circuit by which we could turn the apple off and on. Another circuit was attached to an element inside the apple to cause a smoking effect. The entire setup was governed by timing wires attached behind the apple and away from the camera. In addition, we built an applesauce thrower that would toss the applesauce wherever it had to go. We knew there would be a lot of takes, so we literally went through hundreds of apples in an effort to keep all of them looking like the same one. Someone would have the job of just sitting and sorting through apples all day.\" The gun is put to another test when a fly ball hit by next-door neighbor Ron Thompson (Jared Rushton) breaks the attic window of the Salinski house and triggers the dormant shrinking cannon. \"We built a baseball gun specifically to break that window,\" said Chesney, \"Originally it was made for Stuart Gordon who wanted to see a master of the ball being hit by the bat and soaring through the attic window. So we knew it had to be accurate enough to go through a window from a distance of seventyfive to one hundred feet. Essentially it was a large compressed air cannon with a six-foot barrel. It was very powerful. At full force, it could throw a baseball through a three-quarter-inch piece of plywood at fifty yards. Nothing could hold up to it. When Joe Johnston came on board, the sequence was restoryboarded into five or six different cuts, so the baseball gun was not needed for the window-breaking shot. Instead we designed a small, but accurate slingshot - borrowing the shrinking cannon's laser as a sighting device - to throw the ball at slower speeds and bounce it around reinforced parts of the set.\" Activated by the baseball, the shrinking cannon runs amuck, zapping everything that crosses its video monitor - including Szalinski's tattered 'thinking couch' and chair. But instead of demolishing the furniture as it had the apple, the device miraculously reduces both to dollhouse-sized furniture. The shot was achieved rather simply by photographing the couch and chair on the attic set and then removing them from the scene for a second take. Pete Kuran and his animators then bridged the two with some rapidly diminishing cel animation that shrank the furniture to near invisibility in just a few frames. When Nick Szalinski (Robert Oliveri) and Ron Thompson go to the attic to survey the damage done by the baseball, they too are zapped and reduced to miniscule size. Moments later, they are followed by their teenage siblings - Amy Szalinski (Amy O'Neill) and Little Russ Thompson (Thomas Brown) - who also fall victim to the miniaturization process. \"The shrinking shot was designed to begin wide on the kids and then truck in on them as they shrank down,\" said Fichter. \"Since we did not have a motion control camera in Mexico, we tried shooting the plate on the set using just a dolly track. The scene had to begin wide on the attic and then move in tight on a floorboard. The dolly was giving us some movement problems, however, and the results were unsatisfactory. In the end, the set was transported back to L.A. and filmed on stage at VCE under motion control. The kids were photographed separately in front of a bluescreen - standing on a mylar floor so that the blue was reflected all around them. We used a motorized zoom to zoom in on them at various speeds. Because the lens could only take them down to a certain size, Pete Kuran took our footage and reduced the kids even further via an optical zoom.\" Shrunk to the size of pinheads, the children suddenly find themselves on a giant attic floor covered with dirt, dog hairs and dust balls. \"The floor was a huge forced-perspective set built out of wood - a fan of boards that got narrower as it continued towards the back,\" explained Fonseca. \"We rigged it on a platA trickle of water becomes an impassable river for the tiny foursome. Rather than transport forty-foot blades of grass to an actual river, a tabletop set was constructed and the kids matted in. Their reflections were produced during the miniature shoot with still photo cutouts held on sticks above the water. / In an attempt to determine their location within the backyard jungle, the three boys climb a flower stem to have a look around. A giant flexible stalk was constructed by the art department and rigged by Peter Chesney, Filmed against a bluescreen, the kids and the stalk were later composited into a background plate of the Szalinski house. CINEFEX 41 • 43"],["44","Honey, I Shrunk the Kids","While climbing the flower stalk, Nick loses his grip and blossom. Moments later, a giant honeybee descends and begins gathering pollen. Nick is gathered up with it and Russ jumps on the back of the bee to try and help Chesney and company. / Wider shots - accomplished during postproduction at Perpetual Motion Pictures - employed a puppet bee and puppet kids animated with a combination of stop-motion techniques. Camera operator Jo Carson studies a setup. falls onto an adjoining him. Portions of the sequence were achieved with a mechanical bee constructed by Peter and motion control 44 • CINEFEX 41 form at a ten-degree angle - starting at two feet and going to twelve feet in height. The wood was sprayed with urethane foam and sculpted to simulate the grain and imperfections seen in an attic floor at that scale. A final coat of fiberglass resin provided a rigid surface upon which the kids could walk and run.\" In a state of shocked disbelief, the children survey their situation and observe with fascinated revulsion the gruesome sight of a dead fly lying on the dusty attic floor. For creature creator David Sosalla - a veteran of both Apogee and ILM - it represented his first opportunity to fabricate a Volkswagen-size insect. \"The body was eight feet in length and cast from fiberglass. It had a wingspan of twelve feet and the wings were made from vacuform plastic. We covered the back end with a reflective cellophane to simulate the iridescent abdomen of a real fly. It was Quark - the family dog - tries to alert his master to the terrible accident; but by the time Wayne Szalinski reaches his attic retreat, the baseball has toppled off the gun, deactivating it. Wayne notes the broken window, but is oblivious to the miniscule children shouting in voices too faint to be heard and jumping up and down at his feet. \"The giant legs and feet actually belonged to the second unit assistant director,\" said Fonseca. \"They were photographed full-size and composited optically with a bluescreen shot of the kids.\" Presuming the machine to be a hopeless dud - and depressed by ridicule from his peers - Szalinski takes out his frustrations by smashing the device to pieces, sending a shower of parts crashing down around the children. The hysteria escalates when the inventor - having vented his anger - sheepishly grabs a broom and begins to clean up the mess he has made. The children flee in terror as the giant broom chases them back and forth across the attic floor. Unnoticed by the giant adult, they are ultimately swept into a dustpan and deposited into a plastic trash bag. Comprising the sequence was a combination of bluescreen shots of a real broom chasing the tiny foursome intercut with noncomposite shots of them dodging a giant prop broom. The prop broom - weighing in at just under a thousand pounds - equaled about one square inch of a normal-sized broom. The bristles were one-foot-diameter cylinders cut from large blocks of foam by lead sculptor Daniel Miller. Each strand was attached to a steel grid which represented the head of the broom and the entire construct was supported by a steel framework that hung from the rafters above the set. To simulate the sweeping motion of a broom in action, four cables were attached to the gridwork and operated by a crew of eight. \"It worked very much like a crossbow,\" explained Chesney. \"We constructed the broom to swing the whole length of the stage - in an elliptical path to keep it flat on the ground as long as possible. The frame was pulled back against a climbing ascender - which is basically a rope clamping device. The slack was pulled in and a trip release allowed it to swing out and back. It was really big - like a double-decker English bus swinging across the stage. A flying counterweight set against an opposite set of ascenders locked the broom when it completed its path and prevented it from swinging back - until we got used to using it and realized there was no safety problem in letting it swing free.\" In addition to constructing the broom, it was up to Chesney to ensure the safety of the children as they were being swatted about. To this end, he engineered a harness that was capable of suspending the young actors comfortably for long periods of time. When the children were running from the broom, they were fitted into the harnesses and supported from above by piano wire which ran all the way to the ceiling. If the broom struck the actors - advertently or not - they could fall down only six inches and then would be supported by their harnesses and the broom rig would just push them along rather than run over them. \"The kids loved being in the harnesses,\" said Chesney. \"They were like mini costumes that fit underneath their clothing. The design was based on a mountain climbing harness and used nylon webbing and velcro. The velcro banded the children at the ankles, below and above the knee, and at the thigh while the webbing carried all of their weight. Rather than hanging the actors by the thighs, the stress lines were designed to run all the way to their heels. We made metal fittings at the hip for swiveling - pivot-pointing the center of gravity about an inch above the hip. For shots where the kids are actually in the broom hanging onto the bristles, the actors were on wires attached directly to the broom. It was very safe. You could fly a three-hundredpound adult with all the wires we were using on them.\" Chesney and his crew also needed to devise a means of producing air turbulence as the broom swept across the floor. \"Electric fans have horsepower limits for large work, so we looked into gasoline engines. Churubusco had a couple of giant DC-3 airplane engines to produce hurricane-type effects, but they had not been turned on in a couple of years. They were extremely heavy and the exhaust was terrible - they were also loud. So we decided to build our own based on small ultralight aircraft engines with a very high horsepower-to-weight ratio. Then we found some high-temperature exhaust tubing to run the exhaust off the stage and out the door so there would be no contamination of the air on set. Three or four guys could pick the whole thing up and move it around the set comfortably.\" In addition to the attic floor, the broom sequence also involved a number of lesser sets. \"We built a really large section of the dustpan on a forty-five-degree incline,\" said Fonseca. \"That was used for scenes of the pan being tipped into the top of the garbage bag with the children and debris sliding into the bag. It was a very large wooden set with multiple curves that copied the shape of the actual dustpan used in the live-action. We went through a process of buying every dustpan in Mexico City - at least thirty different kinds - and of the thirty, we chose our final design on the basis of several parameters which included color and size and its visual relationship with other objects. The finished surface was puttied with Bond-o to make it very smooth and then hand-rubbed with multiple coats of lacquer to simulate plastic. The kids flew off the pan onto mat-"],["45","Honey, I Shrunk the Kids","CINEFEX 41 • 45"],["46","Honey, I Shrunk the Kids","46 4 CINEFEX 41"],["47","Honey, I Shrunk the Kids","tresses and into the arms of our stunt coordinator. In between each take, the dustpan slide was cleaned off and then pastewaxed by hand and buffed so there would be no stopping the kids until they hit bottom.\" A second set piece was built for a connective shot of the children falling headlong from the edge of the dustpan into the gaping darkness of the trash bag below. \"We built just the very bottom edge of the dustpan so that the stunt doubles - along with dirt and debris - could literally drop forty feet through the air into a sixty-foot-tall trash bag. There was an army of people on scaffolding up above who were throwing giant electronic components and screws and stuff into the chute of the dustpan. Even though they were lightweight mockups, the larger trash pieces which might have posed a hazard to the kids were attached to cables so that they stopped just before hitting the bottom while the kids slid on by. We struggled with ways to give the bag thickness and shininess - also scale. The solution was to spray a thin layer of urethane foam onto black vinyl.\" A new world awaits the children as they slice their way out of the trash bag which has been deposited out in the backyard. Between them and the house is a lush green landscape filled with forty-foot blades of grass. One of the few matte paintings in the production - a rendering by artist Syd Dutton of Illusion Arts - was employed for the establishing wide shot of the fence and partial trash bag. With thoughts of their parents and rendezvous at the mall to spur them on, the tiny children venture forth into the unknown by first sliding down a giant blade of grass to reach the ground. A few steps into their journey, the adventurers are alarmed at the sight of a monstrous form sweeping overhead and they run for cover under a nearby toadstool. The disturbance turns out to be nothing more than a harmless butterfly - with a wingspan calculated by Nick to be forty-two feet at their scale. \"Actually it had a wingspan of eighteen inches,\" Sosalla remarked. \"The wings were cable-operated and flocked to produce a fuzzy texture similar to the wings on a real butterfly. The model was essentially a static object filmed in front of a bluescreen.\" A bit further up the trail, the children are startled once again - this time by a giant tyrannosaurus rex lying in wait. It takes them only moments, however, to realize that the prehistoric beast is simply a toy that Nick has left out in the yard. The giant plastic plaything was created as a simple insert by Phil Tippett and company. Essentially a Revell model kit, the dinosaur was detailed with clay to create added dynamics and then photographed in a miniature set. Working within the giant backyard set proved to be an adventure for cast and crew alike. The geographic irregularities of a real lawn had been replicated with such precision that very little flat ground remained where dolly tracks could be laid. \"Because of the terrain,\" said Johnston, \"we shot probably ninety percent of the backyard set in fifteen to twenty percent of the area. The camera was almost always in the same general location - but by looking in different directions and bringing in loose blades we could make each shot appear different.\" Actor Robert Oliveri was placed in a giant prop blossom and photographed against a bluescreen for eventual incorporation into miniature background footage of the hovering stopmotion bee. / A wild ride ensues when the bee takes off with the two youngsters clinging to its back. After circling the yard, the insect buzzes Wayne Szalinski who tries to fend it off with a baseball bat. / Stop-motion animator Laine Liska manipulates the twelve-inch puppet and its tiny riders. Gross movements through the frame were produced by motion control operator Richard Malzahn who worked closely with Joe Johnston in choreographing the insect aerobatics. To create a blurring effect, electronic wings were programmed to beat eight times during each foursecond exposure. / To provide plate material for some of the low-level bee shots, a motion control camera with a snorkel lens was flown over a small set filled with four-foot blades of simulated grass. CINEFEX 41 • 47"],["48","Honey, I Shrunk the Kids","Supported by wire-rigged safety harnesses, actors Robert Oliveri and Thomas Brown cling precariously to the giant mechanical bee while wind machines blow mechanical bee - a joint project of the Peter Chesney and David Sosalla units - incorporated thorax and abdomen sections cast in fiberglass and then covered with thousands of fiber Articulation was provided through hydraulic actuators. photography Rick Fichter records a bluescreen shot of Brown for a scene of Russ leaping onto the bee in an in their faces. The optic hair strands. / Effects director of effort to save Nick. 48 - CINEFEX 41 As the production progressed, Rick Fichter was losing ground in his struggle to keep up with the voluminous effects work. \"I found myself being pulled from one set to another to advise or assist in shooting plates. There were simply not enough skilled people to go around. I began using our interpreters as extra assistants. Things were definitely getting out of hand. The workload was killing us and there was no time to do any maintenance on the cameras. Joe Johnston understood our dilemma, but he was also under the gun. This was his first picture - and a big one at that.\" After convincing Disney of the urgency of getting relief, Fichter contacted gaffer Mike Maley back in California and persuaded him to join the team. \"We also brought on a second American assistant and two additional Mexican assistants. By the time Mike arrived, I had fallen ill and lost twenty pounds. Every night I was so tired after being on the set all day that instead of eating dinner back at the hotel dining room, I would buy something from a street vendor on the way. I knew it would With reinforcements on board, a considerable amount of pressure was lifted from the effects unit - until new problems arose with lighting the bluescreen that would be needed for an abundance of traveling matte shots. \"It was a huge bluescreen,\" stated Fichter, \"about forty by seventy feet. We could not afford to light the screen with fluorescents, so we experimented with various combinations of paint surfaces and gels and light intensities until we came up with something workable. Ultimately we used a bunch of incandescents that belonged to Churubusco - about fifty-five 10K units and forty-five 5Ks. We also brought in nine heavy head arcs - four of which were used on the screen and the rest on the subjects in front. Unfortunately there were spikes in the voltage which made the reciprocal rate vary - which in turn created a flicker on the bluescreen that we did not notice until months down the line.\" A mere trickle of water on the ground becomes a muddy river as the youngsters pause to reflect on their perplexing predicament. One of them tosses a stone into its murky depths. \"There was actually no river in the shot,\" Smith revealed. \"At one point we had considered taking our giant blades of grass down to a real river and setting them up to get the shot, but we decided it would be too expensive in the end. Instead we built a miniature version of the river that was about as big as a billiard table. A live plate was shot of one of the children standing on stage throwing a rock -which simply bounced on the floor. The splash was created by firing a BB gun into our miniature river. David Sosalla took photos of the kids to scale which were then made into cutouts and held on sticks above the river to create reflections in the water. A line was rotoed around the edge of the plate to create a fake shoreline. The shot looks so flawless that I have yet to meet anyone who knew it was an effects shot.\" To make the scene even more convincing, Sosalla provided yet another dead insect that was shown floating down the river on its back. \"Inside the insect was some yellow gook that Dave squirted out - it was pretty disgusting.\" In an effort to locate their houses, the boys attempt to get a bird's eye view by scaling a sixty-foot flower to look around. Live-action footage was accomplished with the young actors climbing a giant flexible stalk built by the art department and rigged by Peter Chesney, \"We controlled the pivoting of the stalk with three block-and-tackle devices in a triangular overhead support arrangement that could move the top of the stalk in a fifteen-foot circle. The base was a multi-rope sling-type pivot - a floating knuckle. As with everything else on the film, safety was a major concern. When the kids were climbing on the upper portion of the stalk, we had them on harnesses which were wired either into the stalk or the ceiling.\" As the pint-size adventurers near the top, the stem begins to sway from their accumulated weight and Nick is tossed onto a neighboring flower. \"The blossom Nick falls into was made from a stretched plastic lit from underneath to give it a transparent look,\" explained Johnston. \"The original flower produced for the scene was built as a trampoline - the kids were going to jump up and down on it until they finally reached a height where they could see the house. Technically, everything worked quite well - the problem was it was ugly. It was supposed to have been this light, airy flower, but the thing looked like it weighed a hundred tons. Our decision to make a new one was based partly on our desire to simplify it, partly to save money and partly to make it look more interesting.\" As he struggles to extricate himself from the blossom, the allergy-prone Nick is alarmed to find his hair and clothing covered with clumps of sticky pollen. All thoughts of allergies disappear, however, when moments later an elephantine honeybee approaches and hovers over the flower on which he is stranded. As the insect proceeds to gather the blossom's pollen with its legs, Nick is inadvertently gathered up along with it. Russ - still on the nearby stalk - leaps onto the back of the bee and grabs ahold of his imperiled companion as the insect takes to flight. The ensuing sequence employed a combination of fullsize mechanical and miniature stop-motion effects. Live-action footage - involving both Robert Oliveri and Thomas Brown - was accomplished atop a giant bee abdomen and thorax produced by Image Engineering. \"The mechanical bee consisted mainly of a solid thorax cast in fiberglass with an articulated abdomen cantilevered off the back,\" said Chesney. \"To support the weight of both the bee and the children, Bruce Hayes - my lead mechanic - and I built a hydraulic serpentine armature based on a single hydraulic cylinder inside the abdomen. Also inside the abdomen was a 'backbone' composed of seven links mechanically cross-linked with an accelerating curvature. These mechanical vertebrae were then attached to corresponding upper and lower hard exoskeleton plates that would slide and move just like on a real bee. The skin - made by Dave Sosalla's crew - was constructed from fiberglass plates and covered with hairs that were actually dyed optical fibers.\" Aware that the honeybee encounter would entail a major postproduction effort, Tom Smith scouted out a number of prospec-"],["49","Honey, I Shrunk the Kids","tive effects houses and eventually assigned the sequence to Perpetual Motion Pictures - a young company located in the Los Angeles suburb of Pacoima. Not only did Smith find a bee team at Perpetual Motion, he also found the perfect spot to accomplish the bulk of his postproduction effects work - right next door in one of the light industrial complexes ubiquitous to the area. Six working bays and a storage bay were leased for the duration. Two would be designated as bluescreen stages. Another would be equipped solely for stop-motion work. The remaining three would be workshop areas for the construction of models and miniature sets. It was an early decision that most of the bee footage would be accomplished using a stop-motion puppet photographed in a bluescreen environment for insertion into live-action plate material recorded in Mexico. In what would prove to be the crowdpleasing highpoint of the film, Nick and Russ hang on for dear life as the bee makes a barnstorming run through the Szalinski backyard - banking off a fence, passing a birdhouse, buzzing a barbecue and a set of lawn chairs and then finally barreling down a clothesline through a pair of boxer shorts and into the startled faces of Wayne Szalinski and Big Russ Thompson. Rick Fichter was tasked with supplying the dizzying plate material. \"I was very excited about the bee sequence - I thought it would be dynamite. Originally it had been felt by the crew that we would use Vistavision for the plates - but I had my doubts about that. Because Vistavision cameras are so heavy, the only way we could shoot low enough to actually scrape the grass and things would be to use a snorkel lens or a ninety-degree prism. Even that would not have given me the freedom to roll the camera as much as I wanted. Maximum mobility was essential. Ultimately, I ended up using an Arri with a Super-35 gate and a handle attachment so I could hand-hold the camera and run with it during some of the shots. If I had used a Vistavision camera, I would never have had that kind of control.\" Fichter began with a series of closeups from the bee's point of view showing actors Rick Moranis and Matt Frewer attempting to dodge out of the way and fend off the airborne attacker with a baseball bat. \"We opted to use wide-angle lenses for the sequence to make any jarring movements less detectable. I was very familiar with the lenses and could pretty much tell what the camera's POV would be without actually looking through the viewfinder. I had a clamp on top of the magazine to serve as a handle and there was a video tap on the camera which added some extra weight. When I got running with it, the momentum was so great that I could not stop and I actually ran into the fence a few times. There were scars on me for weeks.\" Though running with a hand-held camera was adequate to record some of the plates, Fichter soon realized that a crane of some sort would be needed to accomplish much of the workload. \"At first we got a parachute harness and tied me to the end of a construction crane about seventy feet off the ground. I got some interesting footage that way, but I had very little control over what I was shooting. So we decided to use a Tulip crane - CINEFEX 41 • 49"],["50","Honey, I Shrunk the Kids","50 - CINEFEX 41"],["51","Honey, I Shrunk the Kids","which is not my favorite piece of equipment because it can be unstable in certain kinds of arcs - but it was the only thing we could find in Mexico. On the end we put an extension designed for a remote camera mount and then, beyond that, a platform that I could lie on to shoot. We knew it was dangerous because the extension was designed only to support a camera - not an operator - but we needed the extra reach and that was the only way we could get it with the equipment on hand.\" The jerryrigged system seemed to work. Strapped onto the floating platform, Fichter was able to hand-hold his camera while members of his crew swung the crane through a succession of carefully choreographed maneuvers. The footage was superlative, but good fortune would not be with them throughout the shoot. \"It was late in the day and we were losing light. We were doing one of our incredible threehundred-and-sixty-degree sweeping moves and we had been getting so good at them that we failed to rehearse this one as thoroughly as we should have. During the middle of the shot, one of the guys on the ground got in the wrong position, and when he tried to correct it the crane locked up. Suddenly the whole world went topsy turvy. I could hear people yelling and I knew I was falling, but I was tied to the crane and unable to move.\" Some of the crew was knocked to the ground as the top-heavy crane collapsed in a heap. During the fall, Fichter rolled over the edge of the platform and would have been crushed by it had not camera operator Mike Maley responded quickly and thrown a strategic body block. \"Mike saved my life. My major worry was that the camera was going to get trashed. I injured some ligaments in my foot and knee. We knew we were playing with a dangerous piece of equipment, but we could not get anything else unless we had something shipped down to us out of L.A. - and that would have been an incredible expense.\" Hazards notwithstanding, the footage was ultimately completed and delivered to Perpetual Motion where the stop-motion bee would be incorporated into the subjective flight material. A twelve-inch stop-motion bee was designed and armatured by David Sosalla. Legs, antennae, head and thorax were made fully positionable to afford the greatest possible latitude of movement. The body was cast from foam and covered with latex in which tiny hairs were imbedded. \"The fuzzy hair on the bee was made from the same material used in making nylon broom bristles,\" said Sosalla. \"Each bristle was heated, pulled apart and applied individually - thousands of them in the outer layer of skin.\" Wings were engineered to duplicate the pulsating rhythms of a real bee. \"Each wing had its own separate motor which - combined with the animation - produced a blurring movement. To simulate the transparent quality of a real bee's wings, we used a thin vacuformed uvex plastic to which a subtle vein pattern was applied. The wings were so clear that we had to hit them with a dulling spray in order to see them.\" Like-scale puppets of Nick and Russ were also produced. Once completed, the models went next door to Perpetual Motion where the resident bee team - consisting of animator Laine Liska, motion control technician Richard Malzahn and camera operator Jo Carson - would spend the next several months laboring on the sequence. \"The combination of traditional stop-motion and go-motion worked really well for us,\" noted Malzahn. \"By using motion control, we were able to keep the wings beating and the body constantly moving throughout the exposure. Originally we were given about seventeen shots - although that number increased somewhat as the sequence expanded. Joe Johnston is very visually oriented and he would come in almost every morning and we would look at the footage together. Joe would take the black-and-white reference footage of the background plate and go through it frame by frame and make little 'x' marks where he thought the bee should be in each frame. When it was played back, the movement was rough, but the basic idea was conveyed. Then I would take that footage back to the roto camera and shoot a clip for the viewfinder so that on stage I could essentially follow the dots. Usually I would have a move completed in about two hours. On the average we were able to produce about one shot per day.\" The opening shot in the sequence proved to be the most difficult for Laine Liska. \"It was one of the few scenes we had to do over. Joe Johnston had originally wanted the bee to land on the flower. Because of the logistics involved, the results looked somewhat mechanical. I had a swarm of bees living near my house at the time; and in the process of studying them, I noticed that bees do not necessarily need to land on a flower in order to gather pollen. Sometimes they kind of hover over it in midair. So we decided to have our bee hover, but still reach down and pick at the pollen. The bee had to actually pass the puppet Nick from one leg to the other to get him into position so he could be picked up with the pollen, so we ultimately ended up shoving the character into a fairly large miniature of the flower. Three cuts later, the puppet Nick had worked his way up to the side of the bee's body.\" Getting Nick into position provided Liska with an opportunity to demonstrate his animation skills. \"There was an entire issue surrounding the transition of getting the puppet Nick up on the bee,\" recalled Malzahn. \"Since Russ had already jumped onto the back of the bee from a flower in a previous scene, we decided to have Laine animate the Russ puppet reaching down and pulling the Nick puppet up onto the side. All of the shots with the animated bee were done using the puppet humans except for the beginning shot. For that one, we composited an over-the-shoulder bluescreen element of the animated bee with a live-action plate of the actor sitting in the Nick was done using the puppet.' As the sequence progresses, Nick and Russ cling precariously to the bee as it soars around the yard. Long shots featuring the puppet children were matched to the action transpiring in adjoining live-action cuts of actors Robert Oliveri and Thomas Brown hanging onto the mechanical bee. \"Live-action scenes of the kids holding on with one arm were done using wires,\" stated Liska. \"The actors were actually floating in the air. We did a simBluescreen footage of the actors on the mechanical bee was composited over live-action plate material shot by Rick Fichter in Mexico. Fichter accomplished the dizzying plate work by running around the Szalinski yard with a hand-held camera and by strapping himself perilously onto the end of a swooping camera crane. / The puppet footage was also photographed against a bluescreen and incorporated into live-action plates. / When a lawn sprinkler is accidentally turned on, the children are bombarded by giant drops of water. Peter Chesney concocted a fluid mixture of water and methylcellulose, and devised an air cannon system to project it in forty-gallon bursts. Actress Amy O'Neill dodges one of the giant drops. For scenes in flight, the water drops were inserted as an optical distortion effect by Visual Concept Engineering. CINEFEX 41 • 51"],["52","Honey, I Shrunk the Kids","appears to be a menace - children and even permits itself to be used as a beast approaches were employed requiring full mobility were accomplished in miniature with stop-motion animation provided by David Allen. / pulled along on a makeshift sled, live-action plates were mechanical saddle mounted matte was used to eliminate waistline and replace it with unobstructed background imagery. The modified plate was then projected onto a rear process screen while keeping the puppet human legs carefully aligned to the live-action torso. The result An ant - which at first eventually befriends the of burden. Several to produce the insect character. Wide shots For scenes in which Ron Thompson rides the ant while the other kids are photographed with actor Jared Rushton atop a on tracks. A split-screen everything below his Allen animated the ant, was a live performer apparently riding a stopmotion character. 52 - CINEFEX 41 ilar move with the puppets using aluminum wire which was run underneath the bee's skin and connected to the arm and the torso of the puppet Nick. With that setup, we were able to duplicate the action in the live shots as Nick flew through the frame on the back of the bee. It was Richard and his motion control that really gave character to the shots. He controlled the bee's movement and its position within the frame. All I had to do was keep the body at the right angle, the legs moving in the right direction and animate the puppet humans to make it look like they were real. The background is what made the scenes work. It was a little difficult watching the footage of the bee in the dailies because sometimes we were changing directions every four or five frames - even flying backwards - because that was what the background required.\" Shooting was not without its problems. The delicate wing mech nics were particularly susceptible to failure. \"To create the blurring effect we wanted,\" said Malzahn, \"we shot a four-second exposure which allowed eight wing beats per frame. Each time the wing fluttered, a small flexible drive shaft turned - and the problem was that they would tend to overheat and wear out. We had to pump air into the bee to try and keep everything cool. Every time we blew a drive shaft, we would lose a day of work. Finally we solved the problem. Instead of using the wing movements during our black-and-white tests - as we had previously done - we used them only when we shot the final scene. That helped a lot.\" In certain instances - specifically some shots of the bee whizzing through tufts of grass - the Perpetual Motion team shot not only the insect and its riders but also the background plates. \"Shooting the plates was tricky because we were locked into the timing of the animation,\" explained Liska. \"We had animated the bee first and then eight weeks later photographed the background that it would go into. Each element had to be shot in layers as separate foreground and background plates because the bee was seen flying both in front of and behind blades of grass. For the beginning shot of the bee diving through the grass, we dug an outside trench in about twenty feet of real grass and then flew a camera with a snorkel attachment through it.\" To make the bee's flight pattern appear as authentic as possible, Malzahn programmed a reactive bump into the shot whenever the bee was supposed to graze one of the blades of grass. Liska would then animate each blade frame by frame to match the hits. \"In one of our shots, Joe wanted to have the grass look like it was blowing. We had a small set with some fake grass in it - about four feet tall - so we were able to produce the effect by wiring the grass and actually stop-motion animating individual blades of it using a traditional screw system - tightening the wires a little at a time.\" Most of the fifty or so composites employed in the sequence were assembled at Illusion Arts by optical printer operator Mark Freund and lineup technician Mark Dornfeld. \"The shots were complicated by the fact that various elements were not always shot in correct scale,\" stated facility manager Bill Taylor. \"A shot in the beginning of the sequence required a composite of miniature petals with the core of the full-size flower that the real Nick was sitting on. The miniature petals were not photographed to correct scale so we had to correct the scale to match that of the full-scale flower and then composite the shot with the bluescreen animated bee.\" After their spin around the yard, Nick and Russ are thrown from the bee into the grass - dazed, but unharmed. Meanwhile, in the world high above, Wayne Szalinski realizes the seriousness of the missing children dilemma when he steps on his tiny thinking couch in the attic. Fraught with horror that Amy and Nick - along with the Thompson kids - have been the victims of an accidental miniaturization, Szalinski retraces his steps to the trash bag where the first pieces of evidence were swept into oblivion. Certain that the children have left the bag and are lost somewhere in the grass, he straps a pair of binoculars onto a helmet and stumbles around the yard on crutches, hoping to locate the kids without stepping on them. Instead he becomes entangled in the clothesline and accidentally trips the sprinkler, sending a fountain of water onto the miniature world below. A life-threatening barrage of giant water drops sends the children running frantically through the backyard jungle. \"The sequence went through a lot of changes,\" Johnston recalled. \"In the original script, there was a leaky sprinkler head and a huge river of syrupy water that washes them away. A couple of them are swept off on a blade of grass - another in a bottle cap. One of the kids actually dies. The basic idea sounds great, but I could never see how they were going to shoot the sequence with the budget they had at the time. It was a challenge for us. What could we do that would be as interesting and still a lot cheaper - something that had not been done before? I think it was Tom Smith who said, 'Well, look, we have these raindrops falling — why not make the raindrops like bombs?\"\" After considering computer generated imagery and dismissing it because of expense, Johnston and Smith decided upon an effect combining both optical and mechanical elements. \"We wanted the drops to look like forty gallons of water coming down in a ball. And when they hit, it needed to look like a land mine going off.\" The water explosions were created by Chesney and his team. \"The classic milk drop shape was in the back of everyone's mind. To achieve it, we set up a test procedure to go through a large set of variables such as air pressure, air and liquid volume, liquid viscosity, valve size and funnel shape. Each separate element greatly affected the dynamics of the launched fluid. We designed a test bench air cannon and worked with different funnel shapes on it. Then we started adjusting the viscosity of the water - adding more and more methylcellulose until we came up with a particular thickness that when launched with variable pressures did peculiar things in the air. There were lots of tests and lots of big messes in the street outside our shop at Churubusco. Once satisfied with the look, we went through more than fifteen hundred gallons of the raindrop material during filming.\""],["53","Honey, I Shrunk the Kids","On the set, the young actors were directed to run along a predesignated route as the water bombs struck and exploded all around them. \"To ensure that the kids did not get hurt if they accidentally took the wrong path, we went through a testing procedure with some of my crew who volunteered to get blasted directly by the air cannons. We found it was really hard for anyone to get hurt - they just got wet.\" Not only did the actors and crew get wet - they got slimed. \"The methylcellulose was kind of a problem because it was so slippery. To keep our cast and crew from falling, we glued coarse sixty-grit sandpaper on the bottom of everyone's shoes. Every ten takes or so we would give the kids new sandpaper for their sneakers. It worked great. Fortunately, the cleanup problem was not as bad as we expected. We built vacuum equipment to suck up the slime between takes. Basically we just took care of the big puddles, threw some fresh dirt around everything and kept on going.\" The aerial trajectory and shape of the drops coming down were created optically by Pete Kuran. \"The sequence required huge drops of water to be flying past these kids. All sorts of things were attempted - even breast implants. We tried throwing them and filming them at various speeds, but they did not really form the right shape - they looked more like flattened pancakes with wrinkles in them. Ultimately the decision was made to do it optically. In order to photograph something that nsomething that is normally translucent, you cannot just make blue raindrops and burn them into the shot. You have to create the effect produced by the raindrop. If you hold a piece of solid glass in front of you, you will notice that you are not really seeing the glass, but rather a somewhat distorted image and continuation of the images behind the glass. To duplicate that effect, we had to make mattes in specific shapes that would hold out sections of the frame. Then we burned in the frame again, with the plate image behind the drop slightly distorted. As an added element ed. As an adel or ent of realism, we highlighted one side of the water drop. All of the elements were pieced together under an animation camera and then optically combined.\" In a dramatic scene where Amy falls into a raging river of mud, Chesney combined a mixture of methylcellulose, fuller's earth, nontoxic dyes and food preservatives to simulate the oozy muck. \"There was a lot of recipe experimentation,\" recalled Chesney, \"and in the end, it took us two days just to mix up all of the mud that we needed. There was so much methylcellulose in the mixture that it became about the same consistency as a large jellyfish. Although the tank was only four feet deep, Amy O'Neill's stunt double took a fifteen-foot-fall into it and never even came close to the bottom.\" Russ rescues Amy from the muddy torrent and the trek continues. A foot or two closer to their destination, the bedraggled mini-adventurers - tired and hungry - stumble upon a child's dream come true. Nestled within the towering grass ahead of them is a cookie the size of a house. Joyfully, the children descend like vultures on the discarded sweet, plucking off huge hunks of it and smearing themselves with gobs of creme filling. CINEFEX 41 • 53"],["54","Honey, I Shrunk the Kids","54 - CINEFEX 41"],["55","Honey, I Shrunk the Kids","\"The cookie scene was one of our more difficult shots,\" Fichter commented. \"Joe and I wanted it to look as natural as possible, but building a cookie to scale would have been much too expensive. We tried to get around it by doing a test of the scene from the kid's POV, but Disney insisted that we have a shot showing them actually running towards the cookie. That meant we needed a composite of some sort. Finally we decided on a very old technique that goes as far back as the original King Kong - a combination of rear-screen projection and miniature sets.\" Completed in-camera, the establishing scene required a miniature cookie built in a forced perspective along with elements of a miniature set. \"A fifty-fifty mirror was placed in front of the camera lens at a forty-five-degree angle. The mirror reflected a projection screen set to one side showing a plate of the kids running in the direction of the cookie. Portions of the plate had to be physically matted out to allow the miniature set to come through. Because we were dealing with two different perspectives, the grass in the miniature needed to match the foreground grass in the live-action plate. As we got into it, we discovered that some of the materials used in the miniatures did not match those in the full-size sets - which complicated the lighting. We also needed a tremendous amount of light - not only for the depth of field, but to compensate for the light that was being absorbed by all the greenery.\" With the establishing shot thus accomplished, only a section of the giant cookie needed to be built for the live-action scenes showing the children foraging ravenously on the gooey filling. It is not only the children, however, who are drawn to the cookie. Before long, a giant ant appears. Refusing to abandon their treasure, the kids prepare to fend off the invader. Joe Johnston was responsible for simplifying the sequence. \"In the original script, there was a whole swarm of ants - maybe a hundred of them. The logistics would have been horrible. Animating a hundred ants would have been extremely difficult, and we could not use real ants in the background because they would never have looked like the same species.\" Instead of employing a hundred ants for the scene, only a single ant would be featured. As conceived during the Stuart Gordon preproduction period, the ant was to have been articulated and controlled by an internal operator. Even though the sculpting had been completed and a mold made, Johnston was reluctant to employ the concept. \"It would have been a huge fiasco had we stayed with the original idea. You cannot put a guy in an ant suit for three hours and expect him to still be breathing at the end of the sequence.\" David Sosalla was given the task of reassessing the approach. In the end, three different scales and two different techniques would be used. The largest ant - a nine-foot-long mechanical monstrosity - was radio-controlled and cable-operated for scenes requiring close interaction with the young actors. Its employment was extensive since the ant would eventually befriend and accompany the lost children on one leg of their backyard odyssey. Construction of the ant was relegated to Peter Chesney and company. \"We built the entire body on a balanced-pressure Dave Allen manipulates the stop-motion ant puppet. / Closeup shots were accomplished with a largescale mechanical creation provided by Image Engineering. With a body composed largely of fiberglass and urethane, the insect construct employed pneumatic systems, cable mechanisms and radio control to execute a variety of fluid movements and articulations. / Though the ant was propelled by wheels, a troupe of puppeteers was enlisted to impart convincing leg movements via rod controllers. A waldo system was likewise used to manipulate the expressive antennae. / An elaborate tracking shot as seen from above. / Rider POV shots were accomplished by seating the camera operator directly on the mechanical ant. CINEFEX 41 • 55"],["56","Honey, I Shrunk the Kids","The children bed down for the night in a Lego building campsite. When the friendly ant comes to their rescue, a mismatched opponents. The sequence was accomplished via stop-motion animation at the Phil Tippett facility. Puppets for the sequence constructed by Craig Davies. / Animation assistant Sheila Duignan observes as Tippett orchestrate a segment of the incorporating both the liveaction performers and the stop-motion puppets were achieved using traditional Churubusco, giant cutouts Reference footage with the determining proper scale. / A piece was incorporated into appear to be making direct corresponding Lego piece on block, but their sleep is disrupted when a giant scorpion invades their spirited battle ensues were designed and and Tom St. Amand insect battle. / Scenes split-screen process techniques. / On stage at of the insects aided the actors in establishing appropriate sight lines. cutouts in place likewise aided the animators in three-dimensional Lego one rear-screen setup so that the scorpion would the live-action plate. between the two contact with the 56 * CINEFEX 41 air cylinder and a parallelogram frame so that it appeared to be floating without the usual sloppiness that is characteristic of something so large and mechanical. The abdomen was cast in fiberglass and built on a spring mount which created a natural rhythm throughout the rest of the body movements. The urethane head also had a very precise cable control attached to a three-axis neck which enabled it to go up and down and rotate. The mandibles - also radio-controlled - could open and close and rotate. A separate 'hero' head with enhanced functions was used for closeup shots while an additional 'stunt' version was employed for some of the more athletic requirements. The most expressive component of the ant's physiology was the antennae which, through various movements, helped sug gest the character and emotions of the creature. \"We set up a full waldo cable system using multifunctional joints from the base of the antennae to the tips. For the movement at the base, we installed a two-axis-controlled ball joint which gave us a full radius of left and right movements. In the tip of each antenna was a curled segmented section which was controlled separately. A single puppeteer ran off-camera wearing the waldo. Each arm of the puppeteer controlled the right and left antennae, respectively. If he moved his arms in a certain direction, the antennae would follow his movements. With a simple rotation of the wrist he could make them curl as well. While pains were taken to insure that the mechanical ant was built to accurate specifications derived from the microscopic study of real ants, at times there were exceptions to the rule. \"There was a big issue with the eyes,\" said Johnston. \"Disney wanted to see pupils in them so you could tell which way the ant was looking. We did a couple of tests with painted pupils that made the ant look like a cartoon character. Finally we arrived at a compromise by putting a highlight on the eyes so that you could at least tell that they were moving.\" The voice of the ant was created in postproduction by sound editor Scott Gershin, who used a combination of his own voice and a synthesizer to produce the clicking 'conversation' of the insect. Though the forward propulsion of the ant was accomplished mechanically on a track, it was necessary to suggest its selfmotivation by showing all six of its legs moving appropriately. Hired to accomplish this was the Lemus family - a Mexico City troupe specializing in marionette puppetry. \"Most of the scenes involving the legs in motion were shot from the knee-level up with one puppeteer lying on the floor guiding each leg,\" noted Chesney. \"To prevent the look of a floppy leg, we set up the body attachment geometry with hinges in the vertical and horizontal planes separated by eighteen inches instead of both functions being in a single ball joint.\" For a rough-and-tumble sequence in which the boys attempt to harness the ant for transportation, Chesney had to develop a rig that would enable the actors to mount and ride the uncoop erative insect like cowboys on a bucking bronco. \"For the sake of visually communicating what everyone wanted us to build, we had a tape of Urban Cowboy sent to Mexico City so we could"],["57","Honey, I Shrunk the Kids","CINEFEX 41 • 57"],["58","Honey, I Shrunk the Kids","agree on the movements of the mechanical bucking bull in Gilley's Saloon. Then we engineered similar movements for the thorax of the ant by building a rocking frame attached by offset hard linkage to a spinning counterweight.\" Not only did the mechanism have to provide a bucking effect, it had to also mimic the dizzying circles made by a real bronco. \"The bucking mechanism was built on an eight-foot turntable and spun about by ten puppeteers. As the table rotated, each puppeteer carried out a leg-over-leg sequence with a partner so that each individual leg of the ant would appear to be stepping as it moved. We needed to put the kids into harnesses only when the ant was programmed to throw them off - when we actually jerked them off the ant and flew them into the grass across the stage.\" Though the mechanical ant served admirably for closeup purposes, its dependence upon external cables and puppeteers limited its use for full-view shots. For these, the plan had always been to use stop-motion animation. Veteran animator David Allen - who divided his time between his own small facility in Burbank and special projects coordination at ILM - was engaged to do the work. As with most of the other postproduction effects artists, Allen relocated his essential equipment and personnel to Honey headquarters in Pacoima. By the time the number of ants required for the sequence was reduced to just the one, Allen and company had already produced eight stop-motion puppets. Allen and Tom St. Amand designed the armatures and assembled them from parts machined to their specifications by Conrad Bonderson. Dennis Gordon and David Sosalla then sculpted the basic ant form over the metal skeleton, and from that the necessary molds and castings were generated. Assigned to Dave Allen were four full miniature shots and eight others requiring composites of the stop-motion ant with the live-action performers. \"I was using the traditional method of stop-motion animation,\" explained Allen, \"but instead of animating the puppet like an insect, I tried moving it more like a heavier creature so that it would appear similar to the full-sized mechanical version. When the ant needed to travel quickly during a scene, I would occasionally create a motion blur by attaching a thin wire to the legs and pulling it against the tension of the armature during the exposure.\" Since this was Allen's first attempt at animating an insect, his preliminary research included studying the locomotive habits of real ants. \"Ants will jerk, hop and do just about anything to get around. It was only when I began to study the larger sixlegged insects that I actually saw a precise system of walking. The ant uses an interlocking tripod system, which means that three legs are kept on the ground at all times - the two legs on the outside are balanced by one on the inside followed by two on the inside being replaced by one on the outside. A new tripod position is attained each time the legs come to rest.\" The scenes showing Ron on the back of the ant were the most complex for Allen. The simplest - and most traditional - approach would have been to construct a puppet version of Jared Rushton and animate it along with the ant. Tom Smith and Joe 58 < CINEFEX 41"],["59","Honey, I Shrunk the Kids","Johnston wanted something more lifelike. \"They thought that matting in a real actor would be worth the extra trouble. It was not a new technique. I used it back in 1981 on Caveman for a shot showing Ringo Starr riding on the back of a giant green lizard. The concept itself was devised by Jim Danforth - who at the time was the effects supervisor for that movie. The idea was that a plate recording camera first photographed the actor riding on a mechanical saddle. Then the actor and saddle were removed and a second shot was taken of the background only. Next the lower portion of the first plate was matted out and a portion of the background optically printed in its place. The result was a plate that had the upper half of the actor riding on nothing at all. The stop-motion was done against the second plate using a pair of rubber legs for the actor mounted on top of the lizard. A very soft split was made just below the actor's belt, and the plate of the live actor was optically composited onto the animated lizard. The end result was a live-action character riding atop an animated puppet.\" Though most of the scenes showing Rushton riding the teninch stop-motion ant were taken from fixed camera positions - which facilitated the complex alignment process - one notable exception employed a tracking movement. \"For the liveaction footage, a dolly was assembled in the shape of the ant's abdomen with a sled attached to the rear. Then a plate was made of Jared Rushton as he sat on the dolly and moved through the frame on tracks. The trucking of the camera made lining up the elements between the two plates extremely difficult. The main problem was that no one had gone back and repeated the shot without the actor and dolly. Even without motion control it would have been possible to approximate the move by timing it out with a stopwatch. Consequently - to produce the splitscreen plate we needed - it was necessary to grab sections of the background from frames that were ahead or behind the main dolly action and try to slip them into the plate optically. Bill Taylor at Illusion Arts did a heroic job on it.\" As darkness descends on the neighborhood, Wayne Szalinski and his wife Diane continue the search for their children. Hanging in canvas slings with magnifying glasses and jerry-rigged helmet lights, the desperate parents comb the grass while Big Russ Thompson observes in bemused disbelief. When Russ absent-mindedly flicks a cigarette butt over the fence into his neighbor's yard, the tiny missile creates a fallout of burning ashes and lands in a smoldering heap near the tiny children. Footage of the butt tumbling end-over-end through the night sky was an effect accomplished by Richard Malzahn at Perpetual Motion. \"Paul Gentry made the butt out of a paper towel tube with about sixty wheat bulbs inserted into its end to simulate the glow. We had it hooked up to a rheostat, but it would get so hot that we would have to turn it off between frames.\" The oversize butt was mounted on a rod that was programmed to rotate in an arc similar to what a real cigarette might make if it were flicked by someone. Malzahn photographed it in front of a bluescreen, moving the camera forward as the rig tumbled. \"We combined the bluescreen footage of the falling butt with additional elements showing the fence, the house next door, and the foreground and background grass. An additional plate was shot of the big orange explosion when the cigarette butt hit the ground. All four were composited together after the support rod was rotoscoped out.\" The glow of the soaring cigarette may have gone unnoticed had it not been for yet another minute detail inserted into the live-action master shot by VCE. \"Because the shot was done as a very dark night scene,\" said Pete Kuran, \"Joe Johnston wanted us to add a glow to the end of the cigarette so you could actually tell that it was flying out of Big Russ' hand. We made a very light print of the shot in order to see where the glow was located, then projected it onto an animation camera and - on a separate piece of film - drew in a little spark at the end of the cigarette as it traveled from frame to frame. The whole thing seems kind of simple, but pulling off these subtle gags is often a lot harder than pulling off some that are really splashy.\" The children ingeniously construct torches from the fallen ashes and use them to help make their way across the darkened yard. Creating light and smoke for the glowing ember torches was one of the more difficult practical effects encountered by Chesney and his crew. \"The torches were sculpted out of fiberglass by the art department very late in the production. The segment was being shot in Vistavision with stopped-down lenses for depth of field, so we needed a very bright light to give the effect of glowing embers. We developed two or three different approaches using battery-operated lightbulbs and heating elements with vaporizing oils to produce smoke - but it was beyond the kids' abilities to carry around enough batteries to power them for longer than one take. To satisfy camera requirements, we needed to have a two-hundred-watt quartz bulb, but the outer shell of the fiberglass kept getting burned from that amount of wattage.\" The final effect was achieved using the quartz bulb combined with a red gel placed inside the fiberglass torch. \"We were able to get only one or two takes per set of gels before they would burn clear and create a white spot that would ruin the effect. It took a lot of maintenance manpower to stay ahead of the film crew.\" Exhausted and unable to proceed, the children take refuge for the night in the tubular recesses of an abandoned Lego building block. They are destined to have little rest, however. Before long, their campsite is invaded by a giant scorpion. When the children are threatened by the fearsome beast, the ant they have befriended comes to the rescue. A battle ensues and the scorpion is eventually driven off - but not before the gallant ant has been mortally struck by its adversary's poison stinger. PESE The fight sequence was produced by Phil Tippett - an Oscarwinning veteran of the Lucasfilm effects team who in recent years has split his time between his celebrated alma mater and his own facility in Berkeley. As it had with Dave Allen, Honey, I Shrunk the Kids afforded Tippett his first opportunity to animate a multilegged insect. Although other creatures in the Animator Tom St. Amand manipulates the puppet ant as it scrambles to avoid the scorpion's deadly stinger. Wires taped to the backsides of the artificial grass enabled the foliage to be animated convincingly when either of the puppet characters came into contact with it. / Harry Walton and Tom St. Amand - who together animated most of the shots - maneuver their puppet creations on a bare platform against a rear process screen. Alternating male and female mattes placed between the camera and the puppets enabled selected portions of the plate to appear as though they were in front of the puppets rather than behind. The puppet figures could thereby be sandwiched into the live-action material. CINEFEX 41 • 59"],["60","Honey, I Shrunk the Kids","mour the loss of their ant friend who died trying to dawn comes renewed hope that the kids will be able to cross the remaining stretch normal size. Suddenly the ground begins to rumble and they realize that a power lawnmower is bearing down establishing shot, live-action depict the children fleeing grass-cutting juggernaut. Four solemn children protect them. / With the of yard separating them from the house - and hopefully be restored to upon them. For a wide elements at two vastly different scales were composited together to in terror from the 60 * CINEFEX 41 film were constructed under the supervision of David Sosalla, Johnston gave his former ILM-mate the go-ahead to work alone in generating the materials he would need. Tippett determined that a six-inch ant puppet - about half the size of the one used by Dave Allen - was optimal for his sequence. \"Since we had two creatures to deal with, I wanted to work in a size that would allow us to animate comfortably. Once principal photography had been completed on the large mechanical ant, we were given a great deal of reference material. We also received photos of Dave Allen's ant after it was built. In the final film, all three models appear to be the same ant, but in reality each of them looked very different. I think it's a concept that is ubiquitous throughout the animal kingdom. If you spent a lot of time with a group of collies, you would soon be able to tell the distinct visual differences in each. It's the same with ants.\" The scorpion design was not so straightforward. To construct the puppet, Tippett enlisted Craig Davies who had excelled in designing and manufacturing the ED-209 law enforcement droid for RoboCop, \"Craig designed and built the scorpion from the ground up,\" said Tippett. \"He also did all the fabrication and design work on the ant. Craig found a great book with schematic drawings of scorpions. Ours was a hybrid derived from a number of different species - a tail from one, pincers from another, the body and legs of still another. After we had decided on the design, the body parts were enlarged and composited into one model that had eight legs, two claws and a movable tail. Each leg had five separate joints - which meant there were forty moving parts to animate in the legs alone. We had five moving parts in the backbone, another five in the tail. The teeth - called chelicerae - were tiny crablike claws which had another five joints in them. The legs had to move in a particular configuration with a particular velocity to insinuate that the creature had a certain weight to it. There were literally hundreds and hundreds of moving parts.\" Armatures for the scorpion - also for the ant - were machined and assembled by Blair Clark from designs provided by Tom St. Amand. \"If you watch a real scorpion walk around,\" said St. Amand, \"you will notice that its tail is very stiff. Basically they just hold their claws out in front of them and move around like a machine. We wanted to make the movements of our scorpion more interesting than that. At the same time, we did not want to use the usual big bearing joints for the tail. So I engineered hinges similar to a universal joint - very much like what we had in the feet of the ED-209 armature. Instead of a joint that gives movement in two places, the universal gives us movement from all points originating from the same spot.\" While St. Amand and Clark fashioned the mechanics, Davies began building a prototype that would serve as a sculpting armature. \"The scorpion was sculpted out of sulfur-based clay,\" explained Davies, \"and took about four weeks to complete. After the body was cast from silicone rubber, we took the finished armature and placed it inside the cast shell of the scorpion. A translucent polyurethane elastomer - the same stuff used for making skateboard wheels - was injected into the mold. A yellow pigment was added to achieve proper base color. On a real scorpion, the midsection is like a sac - so to simulate that softness, we cast the model midsection in polyurethane foam. To create the outer plates comprising the exoskeleton, a thin layer of elastomer was painted on the inside of the mold. After the elastomer was dried, we peeled it out, cut it apart and reassembled it into the original mold where its pieces now became the scales of the scorpion. The mold was then injected with foam which chemically bonded both scales and body together. Once that was done, we used thin latex sheets to create membranes between each joint on the legs. To simulate hairs, I implanted monofilament wire into the body - also into my thumb and forefinger.\" No detail was overlooked in perfecting an exact replica of a scorpion. \"I used another elastomer product to give a moist and slimy look to the body. And since a focal point was needed in the eyes, we painted small ball bearings black to pick up reflections.\" Although Tippett himself animated some of the shots, most of the hands-on work was relegated to associates Tom St. Amand and Harry Walton. \"Generally I analyze the first few shots so that I can get a feeling of the kinds of problems to anticipate,\" said Tippett. \"Once I have experienced them myself, I am better able to understand the kinds of difficulties the other animators are going to have since stop-motion animation is a completely physical and mental process. When you are using your muscles to manipulate the creatures, there are physical attitudes that make it impossible for you to move parts. It's like the difference between sitting down playing a piano with your arms in a particular position and being hoisted up on a rope by your feet and still trying to play. The fight between the scorpion and the ant was the most excruciatingly difficult animation job I have ever been involved with. Fortunately for me, Harry and Tom animated about ninety percent of the shots.\" Reference footage was used to help the animators duplicate the complex body movements of actual scorpions. \"Blair Clark found a scorpion at a pet store and brought it back to the shop,\" said Tippett. \"It was a huge black scorpion - about eight inches long. I shot some 16mm footage so we could study it and try to duplicate the proper gait of a scorpion. Ours met a sad end, I'm afraid. As a consequence of the drought California was having at the time, a band of ants got into his cage and killed him - it was terrible.\" The workload was divided into two types of shots - those showing the live-action children interacting with the scorpion and those showing only the two creatures fighting. \"All of the shots involving the kids were basically locked-off as a consequence of using the rear-screen projection process,\" Tippett explained. \"This allowed us to do a number of different types of composites where we could photographically get the model of the scorpion to interact with the children. Most of those shots were treated as long shots that placed the scorpion inside the live-action plate material. We knew that if we were going to have"],["61","Honey, I Shrunk the Kids","any camera movement at all, it had to be during the fight sequence where just the scorpion and the ant were in view. So we worked in moves wherever we could to help free things up and create the illusion that the scene was being shot live-action.\" For the straight miniature shots, a forest setting was created by Paula Lucchesi, Sheila Duignan and Tamia Marg using much of the same materials - wood, urethane foam, plastic and latex - that were used for the oversize backyard set at Churubusco. \"A great deal of time went into the construction of the miniature set,\" said St. Amand, \"and much attention was paid to the placement of rocks and trees and the Lego piece. We wanted to treat the animation the same as live-action, so we took pains to frame the shots in interesting ways. Oftentimes it meant that we had to reach around a tree in order to animate one of the creatures.\" In the opening scene, the scorpion attempts to snatch the children as they run from their nighttime shelter to the protective cover of the dense grass forest. Only Ron Thompson is left behind - trapped inside the Lego tunnel as the scorpion's scissorlike pincers stab at him from without. \"For that shot,\" said Tippett, \"we composited a portion of the twenty-foot-tall Lego piece that was built on the set in Mexico City with a shot of a second Lego built as a miniature by Paula Lucchesi at our shop in Berkeley. The live-action footage was matted in against the stop-motion footage of the scorpion's claws inside the identically colored and perspectively matched miniature. The scorpion is making contact with the Lego - complete with shadows falling across the inside of the tunnel - and both are in the same world.\" A fierce battle ensues when the ant comes to the rescue. \"It was interesting working with both models,\" St. Amand observed. \"Because the creatures were of separate species, each had to be animated differently. The ant was a smaller creature and we decided it had to move faster than the large prop ant built for the live-action scenes because the sequence we were working on had a lot of action in it.\" Certain scenes required all three of the stop-motion animators in order to track and articulate each of the fourteen moving legs. \"If both creatures were in the same shot, we worked it so that each of us would approach the animation table from a different side. That way we did not end up bumping into one another. There were times when the camera was so close to the creature that it was literally coming in contact with the lens shade. For one shot, Phil actually dismantled the scorpion and mounted the claws on the camera in order to get a scorpion's-eye-view of the ant coming towards it from the side of the frame. Another very difficult, but quick shot was one that I animated seen from underneath the body of the scorpion as it is advancing towards the ant. The camera was locked off with the scorpion mounted on a special rig by which we could raise or lower him. When the scorpion was boomed down over the camera and his legs came in contact with the ground, his feet had to be tied in. The shot took six hours.\" Tying down the many legs was an added burden throughout. \"We ran a loop of wire through the bottom of the table into a CINFFEY 41 • 61"],["62","Honey, I Shrunk the Kids","62 - CINEFEX 41"],["63","Honey, I Shrunk the Kids","hole in each foot,\" St. Amand continued. \"After each move, all the feet touching the ground had to be tied off - which was very time consuming. Phil worked out a cycle for the scorpion's legs based on the reference footage he shot of his pet scorpion. From that footage, Harry and I counted the number of frames it would take for the scorpion to lift and lower each leg. The movements would overlap so that hopefully we would not have two legs doing the same thing at the same time. Then we had to work out a different cycle for the ant.\" Motion blurs were created in the time-honored fashion. For scenes showing the scorpion savagely attempting to sting its smaller adversary, Walton would animate the principal puppet moves and then - in the final three frames before its stinger arced to the ground - St. Amand would loosen the tail joints, grab the base of the tail just out of camera range and manually create the required blur. \"It was 'human go-motion, in effect,\" quipped St. Amand. \"Harry used the same technique. Whenever a fast claw action was needed for the scorpion, he would hook a wire to the claw and pull on it during the exposure to create an additional blur.\" The scorpion is finally driven away by the kids who arm themselves with sticks and attack the vicious beast. \"For the last shot where they blind the scorpion, we used a tiny wooden stick that was animated to match the live-action stick. At the exact frame where the stick went into the eye of the scorpion, we had to reach in and physically remove the eye. That was all. Evidently the Disney people did not want to have anything gross come out of it like green goop or blood - although it would have been nice to have a closeup insert of the stick going in to make it more distinct.\" When the sequence was completed as storyboarded and cut into the film, the studio arranged a sneak preview. Many in the audience failed to follow the action. One preview response card asked: \"Why did the big ant kill the little ant?\" Clearly something needed to be done - and for Phil Tippett and his crew, it was both a blessing and a curse to be given an additional thirteen shots to flesh out the sequence. \"Initially,\" Tippett noted, \"the most difficult aspect of the job was making do with only twenty shots to complete the whole sequence. Twenty three- or four-second shots is not much to build a dramatic fight scene. Consequently, we were trying to bridge gaps that could not be bridged logically. Instead of doing one shot to represent one facet of the mosaic that built the sequence, we had tried to create a number of facets all in one shot.\" Most of the new shots went directly into the fight. \"Joe Johnston and I went through and shot animatics of all the added scenes by attaching coat hangers to the models and moving them around in front of a Sony 8mm video camera. We transferred those rough animatics to tape and then to film - and they were cut into the movie and used as a template. The picture could then be scored to the inserts which contained action resembling the scenes we were going to attempt in the revised fight sequence.\" For the additional shots, Peter Kozachik took over for Harry Walton behind the camera and Tom St. Amand returned as principal animator. \"The first added shot came at the very start of the sequence. Initially the opening scene showed the scorpion's pincers coming into the antrum of the Lego tunnel at actor Jared Rushton. We switched that scene with a new wide shot showing the scorpion in front of the Lego. That way the entire body was seen immediately. The idea was to keep the scorpion from being confused with some other creature. We also added a shot of the ant backing away from the scorpion, the ant looking up at the scorpion, a closeup of the scorpion's face, a shot showing the ant dodging the scorpion's tail and a shot of the ant trying to get back up after it had been stung. It was a rare opportunity to be able to go back into the sequence after it had left our hands and add scenes which would hopefully help to clarify it.\" The sequence was made complete with the addition of insectlike sounds created by sound designer Wylie Stateman. \"Wylie was very conscious of not just creating a sound,\" said Johnston, \"but also giving that sound a kind of character. He wanted to work as closely as possible with James Horner, who was scoring the music for the movie. Wylie sent tapes of the sounds used for the shrinking cannon, the bee and the water drop sequence - among others - so that James would know what sounds were going to be used for each effects shot. Once the score was finished, Wylie went back and fine-tuned each of his effects. The process worked very well.\" After a restless night in the Lego, the tiny adventurers continue their cross-yard trek until they are halted by signs of what seems to be an earthquake. The ground rumbles and high winds whip threateningly through the forest of grass. To the childrens' horror, they soon realize that the disturbance is coming from a radio-controlled lawnmower being driven through the yard by one of Nick's friends. As the children run for cover, hundreds of seeds from a dandelion puffball are blown across the frame. \"A real dandelion puffball has about five hundred seeds,\" said Chesney, \"but since the production was not a documentary on the science of scale, we cheated a bit and made about sixty seeds for each ball - with about forty rigged to fly. The idea was for the puffballs to become a part of the ambience of the windstorm.\" Each of the forty seeds was aerodynamically designed and hand-inserted into the stem of the puffball. \"The seed stem was made from balsa wood and paper doweling to keep the weight down. We wanted to create an organic look for the hollow stem of the seeds, so Daniel Miller used stripped ostrich feathers with an iridescent bubbled mylar film glued onto them. Each feather was about three feet long and was weighted just enough so that it would float very, very lightly.\" Upon completion, the puffballs were readied for their flight across the soundstage. \"Each dandelion seed was attached to a single wire going from the opposite end of the ceiling. The only way to get these things to fly in a straight upward line for fifty to sixty feet was to use some rubber band assistance. We needed a rubber band at least twenty-five feet in length that could be stretched to a distance of fifty feet Nowhere within Mexico City could twenty-five-foot rubber bands be found, so Chesney ended In the giant lawn set - constructed by Gregg Fonseca and his crew - the kids run for cover as the lawnmower passes nearby. Wind machines were used to blow dust and pieces of flying grass through the set. / David Sosalla arranges tufts of cotton clouds on an array of thin parallel wires in preparation for an insert shot needed during a reflective moment when the children contemplate their fate while gazing up at the night skies. / Still fleeing the lawnmower, the kids take refuge in a wormhole, but are sucked out by the powerful vortex created by the spinning blades. For vertical shots of the four clinging to one another, the young actors were suspended horizontally from wire rigs and photographed against a bluescreen. CINEFEX 41 • 63"],["64","Honey, I Shrunk the Kids","dog who finally discovers the tiny kids out in the yard. youngsters climb up on his transported directly into the house. When Quark leaps up to the kitchen table where plummets into his father's cereal bowl. The remaining three kids disembark from the dog by running down the bridge of his nose and actors were photographed running off it and onto a flat surface. / The figures were inserted into a plate of the dog and the table. / Nick is nearly eaten for breakfast before Quark and his minicompanions manage to alert It is the Szalinski family Without hesitation, the long furry coat and are the Szalinski parents are about to have breakfast, Nick loses his grip and onto the table. A snoutshaped platform was constructed on the bluescreen stage and the reduced optically and the oblivious adults. 64 * CINEFEX 41 up dissecting bungee cords and using the rubber bands inside for his seed launchers. \"It was like taking apart golf balls. Each recovered twenty-five-foot rubber band was attached to a wire that the camera could not see. So the wire provided the trajectory and the rubber band provided the propulsion. We had forty of them strung all over the stage and it was pretty tricky not getting them in a tangle. On cue, the seeds were released individually - machine gun style.\" With the power mower fast approaching, the children scramble into a wormhole seeking refuge. Clinging desperately to protroding roots and each other, the four form a human chain as the swirling vacuum from the mower above sucks them up inexorably toward its spinning blades. In the nick of time, the adult Szalinskis realize what is happening and yell to the startled youngster to shut off the mower. He does so, and the tiny children are tossed out through the grass chute. Part of the scene was accomplished at Perpetual Motion by Laine Liska and Richard Malzahn. \"The first three kids out of the wormhole were the real kids,\" noted Liska. \"We then animated a puppet Ron Thompson coming out of the hole. In the scene just before they land, all four are stop-motion animated for about twenty-four frames as they streak across the screen.\" For their scenes in the wormhole, the young actors were put in harnesses and suspended from wires in front of the giant bluescreen. \"It was a fairly complicated setup,\" said Fichter. \"Since we needed to shoot above the kids on a crane, we had local stand-ins that we use for tests and rehearsals. After those were accomplished, we put the principals in harnesses and shot with them. Generally, we did not get more than one take at a time, because physically it was very exhausting. By the end of the day, the kids were tired and cranky and were having trouble dealing with being in the harnesses.\" After their ordeal with the lawnmower, the children rejoice when they see the back porch of the Szalinski house almost within reach. Suddenly Quark appears - towering over them. Grasping onto the dog's long coat, the four are at last transported across the remaining yard and into the house. Live-action scenes of the actors hanging onto the dog were accomplished on a large support rig much like the one used for the giant broom. Simulated hairs were fashioned by the art department out of several hundred strands of one-inch-diameter foam rod ched to aten -by attached to a ten-by-sixteen-foot steel grid - another Image Engineering construct. \"A hundred-and-fifty-foot dog is going to have a minimum diagonal turning radius of sixty feet,\" noted Chesney, \"so for the scene where Quark is supposed to stand up with the kids hanging on, we built a sixty-foot aluminum truss to slide the grid on. A crew of eight pulled the grid and truss around using several block-and-tackle mechanisms mounted from the ceiling beams. The truss itself was mounted to a floorpounds - not counting the four children who were in harnesses and wired into the setup.\" En route to the house, Quark brushes off an attack by the Thompson cat. \"Disney originally wanted the cat to chase the dog all over the neighborhood,\" said Johnston, \"through traffic and under the wheels of a bicycle. It would have been a major sequence. The last thing we wanted was another chase scene into some place we had never seen before - which was the intersection of the neighborhood. The instant people begin feeling like they have seen enough is when they start getting bored. We did not want to push our luck.\" Quark scrambles across the kitchen floor and up onto a chair at the table where Wayne Szalinski is about to have breakfast. Losing his grip on the dog hair, Nick is thrown free and plummets into the milky depths of his father's cereal bowl. A brief shot showing Nick falling toward the bowl was achieved with a puppet child - animated at the Phil Tippett facility - falling through the air towards a real bowl of cereal. The actual splash as the boy hits the milk was accomplished live by stunt performer Mickey Cassidy - the eight-year-old son of stunt coorUnable to hear the cries of his son - now adrift in a Cheerio life preserver - Wayne comes closer and closer to spooning the youngster into his mouth. \"We built the Cheerios by spraying urethane foam on tractor tires - Earthmovers - that were twelve feet in diameter,\" said Fonseca. \"We stuck balloons in the foam around the outside of the tires and during the curing process broke the balloons - which gave us the air pockets you see in real Cheerios. Then we took the tubes out and cut each tire in half. Finally they were mounted on a large steel frame built by Peter Chesney.\" Originally the frame had been designed to hang vertically in front of a bluescreen with actor Robert Oliveri suspended from wires on his side. The shot was then going to be turned and optically printed into a real bowl of cereal. \"When we realized the Cheerios could actually float - just like the real things - we opted instead to float them right-side-up in a twenty-five-by-forty-foot aboveground swimming pool filled with four feet of 'milk' made by Peter Chesney's crew.\" Chesney enlisted the help of a chemist to concoct a nontoxic formula in the event any of the ersatz milk was swallowed by Oliveri. \"Since we were using local tap water, we did not want to have anyone getting sick from bacteria in the solution. We ended up maintaining the cereal bowl very much like a swimming pool - except that it was filled with twenty-four thousand gallons of a mixture consisting of methylcellulose, white dye and chlorine.\" Safety considerations extended beyond issues of health. \"If you look at a bunch of Cheerios in a bowl of milk,\" said Fonseca, \"you will notice that they do not all float on the surface of the milk. Instead they kind of sit on top of each other at odd angles. We were dealing with a very small child in an environment where large objects could be qualified as dangerous.\" The Cheerios that little Nick was hanging onto were not the light puffy circles of oats found in most cereal boxes. Four men were needed just to carry one. To preclude any problems of their moving around, those Cheerios in the vicinity of Robert Oliveri were secured to"],["65","Honey, I Shrunk the Kids","CINEFEX 41 • 65"],["66","Honey, I Shrunk the Kids","66 - CINEFEX 41"],["67","Honey, I Shrunk the Kids","the framework. \"We placed Robert in a harness in the center of a Cheerio to inhibit any danger of his slipping beneath the surface.\" With the simulated milk providing a ready soft matte line, the shot of the young actor surrounded by an arrangement of fake Cheerios was optically composited into a shot of a real bowl of Cheerios and milk. For scenes in which Nick is scooped into a spoon and nearly swallowed by his father, Fonseca and company built a portion of the giant silver spoon out of wood and urethane foam. Imperfections which would occur in metal at that scale were sculpted into the surface. When the giant prop was complete, it was sealed with a fiberglass resin and then painted with a metallic paint. In a last-ditch effort to save Nick, the other children pile off Quark by using his snout as a bridge and then run across the table. The disembarkation was conceived very straightforwardly. A background plate of the dog - standing in profile with his nose pressed against the kitchen table - was rotoscoped for exact positioning. Then a ramp was constructed that duplicated exactly the shape of his nose. Next the ramp was placed in front of a bluescreen and the actors were directed to run down it and onto the floor. Finally the two elements were combined optically. For subsequent shots, the art department built the tip of a fork handle which is seen as the kids run across the placemat on the table. The placemat itself was fashioned from a piece of Italian formica and painted pink with gray spots by lead scenic artist Carol Davis. Quark saves the day when he delivers a bite to his master's leg just as Nick is almost to become breakfast. Noticed at last, the shrunken children are transported back to the attic lab in a cereal spoon. Rick Fichter devised a shot showing all four standing in the bowl of the spoon under a magnifying glass. Unable to speak loudly enough for Szalinski to hear them, the children pantomime a baseball game, thus solving the riddle of the defective shrinking gun. The reflection of the actors in the spoon was created by actually shooting the group standing on a mirror. A portion of the resulting reflection was rotoed out of the shot to fit the parameters of an actual reflection of that scale occurring in a real spoon. The real children standing on glass were then optically composited onto a real spoon under a real magnifying glass by Hollywood Optical Systems. Though Salinski believes he has restored and perfected his shrinking cannon, all four parents are understandably hesitant to try it out on their children. Big Russ Thompson bravely offers himself as a test subject. Wayne fires up the machine and Big Russ is indeed zapped down to miniature size - the experiment is a success. For a scene in which Mae Thompson bends down to take a closer look at her tiny husband, actress Kristine Sutherland was actually photographed looking at a miniature figure glued onto a pedestal. \"We bought the figure at a hobby shop,\" revealed Fonseca. \"It even had the correct color clothes. All we had to do was glue it into place.\" The scene was shot using a very short depth of field so that the camera focused on the background figure of the live actress while the foreground miniature figure was kept largely indistinct and out of focus. Moments later, Salinski reverses the shrinking process and successfully restores his neighbor to normal size - give or take a few inches. Another oversized prop spoon section - just the tip - was then used for a shot of the children stepping down from the spoon prior to their own restoration. The same technique used by Pete Kuran for the early shrinking shot was now reversed and reapplied for the enlarging scene. A happy epilogue finds the two families sitting around the Szalinski dinner table as Wayne prepares to carve a roast turkey of gargantuan proportions. On the floor below them, the hero Quark munches on an equally oversized dog biscuit. The edible prop proved fonseca to be a serviceable short order cook. \"Due to some bad weather that put a change in the schedule, we needed the dog biscuit sooner than planned. We had only one chance to make one bone - and it had to be done overnight. For batter, I used ground-up dog food, twelve dozen eggs and fifty cloves of garlic. We used garlic because the dog loved it - he would respond to anything that had garlic in it. After the batter was mixed, it was squeezed into a mold that Daniel Miller had built months before. Then it was taken down to a bakery we had arranged to keep open all night, and the art director sat up babysitting it as it baked for ten full hours. The finished bone was about thirty inches long and weighed close to sixty pounds. The dog loved it so much that it was hard keeping him away from it between takes. Whenever he chomped on it, we had to putty it back together with dog food and garlic paste.\" Even as production was drawing to a close in Mexico, the film still had no satisfactory title. Originally it was to have been Teenie Weenies - a title which no one seemed to favor much. Later it was changed to Grounded. Finally storyboard artist David Lowery solved the dilemma while working out concepts for the traditional crew T-shirt. One of his designs showed four tiny kids looking up from underneath a magnifying glass with a line from the movie, \"Honey, I Shrunk the Kids.\" Few could argue that it was a perfect title for the film. \"Honey, I Shrunk the Kids was a Magnificent Seven kind of movie,\" Tom Smith concluded. \"Instead of having the resources and capital advantage of a big effects facility, we assembled our own crew of very talented people. Some had dropped out of the film business and we brought them back again. Some were in little effects shops that had only done titles and commercials and we gave them their first feature work. Some - like the family of Mexican puppeteers - had never worked on a film before. We brought them all together for this one picture. For me, that was what really made it a great experience.\" Honey, I Shrunk the Kids photographs copyright © 1989 by Buena Vista Pictures Distribution, Inc. All rights reserved. Production unit still photography by Edgar Ladron de Guevara. Additional photographs courtesy of Richard Malzahn and Phil Tippett Special thanks to Howard Green and Steven Rogers. Stagehands prepare for a shot of Nick in the cereal bowl. Giant-size Cheerios were fashioned in the art department by spraying urethane foam onto tractor tires and inserting balloons during the curing process to create characteristic air pockets. The milk - concocted by Peter Chesney - was a semi-viscous blend of water, methylcellulose, white dye and chlorine. / Though Szalinski believes that he has worked the bugs out of his shrinking machine, both sets of parents are hesitant to subject their children to it again. Big Russ Thompson volunteers to be a test subject. Actor Matt Frewer sits on the bluescreen stage during photography of his character's shrinking and subsequent reenlargement. CINEFEX 41 • 67"],["68","Closing pages",""]]});
